tgs:PanelDiscussion.RR_19_DarkTriad a thinkr:Episode, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.PanelDiscussion ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.RealityRoundtable ;
    skos:prefLabel "Dark Triad Personality Traits: How Psychopathy, Narcissism, and Machiavellianism Impact Our Cultures & Social Systems"@en ;
    skos:definition "Nate is joined by psychoanalyst Dr. Nancy McWilliams and forensic psychologist Dr. Reid Meloy to examine the Dark Triad (psychopathy, narcissism, Machiavellianism) and Dark Tetrad (adding sadism) from complementary clinical and evolutionary angles — why these traits persist in the population despite their costs, how they manifest differently in individual relationships versus positions of power, and what real, non-naive guardrails (strong local community, truth-telling, functioning democratic institutions) might do to contain their worst effects at a civilizational scale."@en ;
    dct:created "2025-07-22"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2025-09-17"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    thinkr:hasGuest tgs:Persona.NancyMcWilliams, tgs:Persona.ReidMeloy ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/episode/reality-roundtable-19> ;
    thinkr:hasReplay <https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=XnHSRCN1iAY>, <https://open.spotify.com/episode/6Rg4noyHxfFpgy8ub6jvZH> ;
    thinkr:hasTranscript <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/09/RR-19-Transcript-Dark-Triad-Personality-Traits.pdf> ;
    thinkr:discusses tgs:Concept.DarkTriad, tgs:Concept.Superorganism ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "Complementary rather than adversarial, but distinct in texture from the other RRs — Nancy McWilliams and Reid Meloy defer to and build on each other throughout rather than debate, yet each brings a genuinely different disciplinary lens rather than illustrative texture around a shared thesis. McWilliams opens by explicitly distancing her own approach from trait-based diagnosis ('I don't tend to think in terms of traits, I tend to think in terms of themes'), then supplies detailed clinical material Meloy doesn't have — how she actually works with psychopathic patients in therapy, her critique of the DSM's own diagnostic history. Meloy supplies forensic and evolutionary material she doesn't have — population prevalence estimates, predator/prey framing, extreme case data from his own casework. The one moment of honest gap rather than synthesis: when Nate raises Scott Barry Kaufman's 'Light Triad,' Meloy admits he isn't familiar with the term at all, and McWilliams is the one who has looked it up and explains it. Where the two genuinely converge, they amplify rather than merely agree — when McWilliams calls the entire history of the United States 'somewhat psychopathic,' Meloy doesn't qualify it, he extends it ('the theme of psychopathy has been woven through the history of the United States'). Nate's own contribution goes beyond interviewing: he draws a real cross-disciplinary connection between his own energy/power background and the psychological concept of power ('power in an ecology sense is an ecosystem... accessing an energy gradient per unit time'), and at the close, builds Meloy's closing metaphor (Dark Triad individuals as 'passengers on the train' who must never be allowed to drive) directly into his own recurring Superorganism framing, explicitly flagging he needs to think further about how the two connect rather than presenting it as settled."@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "2026-08-13: fully rebuilt from a complete real transcript + real fetched show notes (MJSullivan's own upload, cross-verified against a direct fetch of the episode's own page), replacing the earlier bare stub built from RR-26's own cross-reference alone. Real, further citable material found but NOT built into this graph in this pass: David Sloan Wilson (real, own TGS episode #56, confirmed via this episode's own real show notes) — a genuine further multi-level-selection/Superorganism connection, deliberately deferred rather than chased down unprompted. 'TGS Episodes 1 + 2' for Zak Stein in this episode's own show notes are ordinal labels for his 1st/2nd appearances (real URLs confirm #122/#180), not literal episode numbers — resolved via direct link inspection, not assumed."@en ;
    .

tgs:PanelDiscussion.RR_10_TheEcologyOfCommunication a thinkr:Episode, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.PanelDiscussion ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.RealityRoundtable ;
    skos:prefLabel "The Ecology of Communication: Moving Beyond Polarization in Service of Life"@en ;
    skos:definition "Nate is joined by four friends and repeat guests — Nora Bateson, Rex Weyler, Vanessa Andreotti, and Daniel Schmachtenberger — to examine communication itself as an ecology rather than a set of facts to transmit: how performative, monocultural, industrial-style language traps people in polarization, and what it might look like to instead seek understanding before being understood, start from commonality rather than the most contested point, and treat silence, context, and not-knowing as real parts of a genuine conversation."@en ;
    dct:created "2024-06-14"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2024-07-28"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.EnergySystems, tgs:Subject.EconomicsAndMonetary ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    thinkr:hasGuest tgs:Persona.NoraBateson, tgs:Persona.RexWeyler, tgs:Persona.VanessaAndreotti, tgs:Persona.DanielSchmachtenberger ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/episode/reality-roundtable-10> ;
    thinkr:discusses tgs:Concept.Schismogenesis, tgs:Concept.Metacrisis, tgs:SchoolOfThought.WarmData ;
    skos:scopeNote "2026-08-13: fully enriched from a complete real transcript + real linked show notes (MJSullivan's own upload), resolving the RR-10 gap flagged since Nora's original RR-26 bootstrap and closing the last remaining built-RR summary gap. Real, further citable material found but NOT built in this pass: Vanessa Andreotti's own real prior solo TGS appearance (#125, 'Hospicing Modernity'); the Dunning-Kruger curve, discussed at length by Schmachtenberger, has no matching Concept individual in this graph yet; Chief Ninawa (a real Indigenous leader quoted via Andreotti) and David Bohm/Krishnamurti (cited directly by Schmachtenberger) are real, deferred citation candidates."@en ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "The most philosophically dense and least topic-driven of the built RRs — four guests who know each other's work well (Nate's own framing: 'you have been on my podcast, many of you more than once') build directly on each other rather than staying in their own lanes, so the discussion reads more like one extended, co-authored argument than four separate positions. Nora opens with the central frame (communication itself as ecology, not transmission); Rex immediately extends it with a direct ecological parallel (a monoculture forest versus a real one); Vanessa reframes it again through a decolonial lens (the difference between 'environment' as something external and 'ecology' as something we're inside of, and how English itself can fail to carry non-Western concepts of land); Daniel closes the opening round by grounding it further in physics and boundary theory (there are no truly separable things, only permeable exchange). None of the four ever pushes back on another's framing — disagreement essentially doesn't happen — but there is real, substantive addition happening in each turn, not just restatement. The one moment of process friction is instructive rather than adversarial: Daniel is candid that dialectical and empathetic communication tools can both be genuinely healing AND weaponized (a fact he treats as unresolved and requiring ongoing discernment, not a settled question), and he shares his own experience running couples counseling as the clearest illustration of how two people's honest accounts of the same relationship can diverge completely. Nate's own contribution is again unusually personal — he opens by admitting he was 'in a foul mood' before the conversation started and closes by directly stating his mood had genuinely lifted by the end, treating his own emotional shift as real evidence for the argument being made rather than an aside."@en ;
    .

tgs:Monologue.Frankly_88_SnowSingularityRocksInTheRiver a thinkr:Episode, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.Monologue ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.Frankly ;
    skos:prefLabel "Snow, The Singularity, and Rocks in the River"@en ;
    skos:definition "BUILT OUT 2026-08-30 from a stub, using a complete real transcript and show notes. Unusually personal and reflective even by Frankly standards — recorded during a real spring blizzard in west-central Wisconsin, Nate opens by naming the real, current chaos of the week (tariffs, Russia/NATO/Ukraine tension, AI) before explicitly shifting register: 'I feel less about explaining and describing, and more about feeling.' Reframes the energy transition not as a question of which technology or fuel source, but of how humans relate to energy, each other, and the natural world — arguing this starts internally, not externally. Directly references a real conversation the prior day with Audrey Tang (Taiwan's former digital minister, already a real, independently-flagged deferred citation elsewhere in this graph), who offered 'plurality' as a real, direct alternative framing to Kurzweil's technological singularity. Also references presenting to the International Federation of Red Cross and Red Crescent's senior leadership (a real organization with 20 million volunteers) the day before, where Nate's own real recommendation was not to try to reform the current system but to cultivate 'rocks in the river' — a small, real number of psychologically prepared, grounded individuals within communities who can act as stabilizing anchors as broader systems destabilize. This episode is the real origin of that metaphor, independently confirmed by RR-26's own real citation of this episode months later."@en ;
    dct:created "2025-03-05"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2025-03-07"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.EnergySystems, tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior, tgs:Subject.EcologyAndEnvironment, tgs:Subject.EconomicsAndMonetary, tgs:Subject.SystemsScience ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/frankly-original/88-snow-the-singularity-and-rocks-in-the-river> ;
    thinkr:hasReplay <https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=QYFfbIY-R88> ;
    thinkr:hasTranscript <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/03/Frankly88TranscriptsSnowTheSingularityandRocksintheRiver.pdf> ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "A real, direct neurochemical reframing structures the episode's core argument: material objects (a couch, an Xbox, a book, a toaster) are described as providing real 'brain services,' with the deeper crisis being an overreliance on dopamine-driven engagement at the expense of oxytocin (bonding, community) and serotonin — 'the ghost of dopamine passed,' a recurring phrase Nate returns to twice. A real, personal disclosure grounds the abstract argument concretely: rather than checking news or messaging his team at 5am during the blizzard, Nate sat with coffee, listened to Brian Eno, and wrote a poem he explicitly declines to share, calling it 'personal and intimate' — offered as a small, lived example of the same inward attention he's recommending to listeners. Real institutional stakes appear directly: Nate discloses presenting to the International Federation of Red Cross and Red Crescent's senior leadership the day before, expanding his usual five-horsemen framework (financial overshoot, geopolitics, complexity, the social contract, ecological degradation) to explicitly include AI governance and the erosion of open-society freedoms as an emergent sixth concern. His own real recommendation to a 20-million-volunteer global organization is notably unhedged: it's too late to reform the current system, and the real task now is cultivating a real, small percentage (his own estimate: 2-5%) of mature, grounded people within any given community who can function as stabilizing anchors — 'rocks in the river' — for when change accelerates. The episode closes with Nate reading, in full, William Butler Yeats's 'The Second Coming' — not reproduced here per this graph's own copyright discipline (see e.g. RR-14's own Gary Snyder citation for the same practice), only the fact and real thematic fit of the recitation: its apocalyptic, chaotic imagery directly mirrors the episode's own opening list of current crises. Closes on an unusually warm, direct sign-off — 'love you all' — distinct from Nate's more typical closings."@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Real, further citable material found but NOT built in this pass: Audrey Tang — already independently flagged as a real, deferred citation in multiple other episodes this graph (Frankly_132's own scopeNote among them), reconfirmed here via this episode's own direct, dated reference to a real conversation 'yesterday.' No dedicated Concept individual exists yet for the 'Five Horsemen' framework (or this episode's own real 'emergent horsemen' expansion of it) — kept as prose in the discussion summary rather than forcing a link to a nonexistent individual; worth considering minting given how frequently this framework recurs across episodes already built in this graph. Brian Eno (real musician, directly named) not built as a Persona — a passing personal reference, not a TGS-relevant citation in the sense this graph otherwise captures."@en ;
    .

tgs:Monologue.Frankly_23_MordorEconomy a thinkr:Episode, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.Monologue ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.Frankly ;
    skos:prefLabel "The Mordor Economy"@en ;
    skos:definition "BUILT OUT 2026-08-30 from a stub, using a manually-cleaned YouTube transcript (MJSullivan's own scrape and cleanup — no official downloadable transcript exists for this episode, matching the same real gap flagged for RR-13 and Frankly_47) plus official show notes. Real, direct origin explanation for one of this graph's own four core economic ScenarioFacets: Nate lays out, in his own words, the biophysical logic behind a 'Mordor economy' — a path where continued economic growth requires directing an ever-larger share of energy and GDP toward procuring energy itself (rising from a real historical low of ~5% in 1999, per Carey King's own research, toward 15% or more) and toward remediating the ecological damage from burning it, leaving less and less surplus for everything else society values. Grounds this in the maximum power principle and Kleiber's Law (organisms' metabolic energy use scaling to the 3/4 power of mass, a scaling relationship Nate argues also holds for national economies), and in Tim Garrett's own real framing of civilization as an energy-dissipating structure. Frames Mordor and the Great Simplification as the two real default trajectories, with the explicit stated goal of the whole show being to change the initial conditions that determine which one a society actually lands in."@en ;
    dct:created "2023-01-27"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2023-01-27"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.EnergySystems, tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior, tgs:Subject.EconomicsAndMonetary ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/frankly-original/23-mordor-economy> ;
    dct:source <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/s/F23-The-Mordor-Economy-Graphs-for-Show-Notes.pdf> ;
    thinkr:discusses tgs:Concept.MaximumPowerPrinciple, tgs:Concept.KleibersLaw, tgs:Concept.GreatSimplification, tgs:Concept.Superorganism ;
    dct:references tgs:Interview.TGS_54_TheStealthAdjustmentOfOil ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "Opens by explicitly naming its own two prior real airings of this idea — the 2021 Earth Day talk, and 'last week' on Art Berman's own podcast — before walking through the logic in full for the first time on TGS itself. The real analytical core is a direct biophysical rebuttal of a 'we'll just get more efficient' techno-optimist response: Nate cites a real, specific figure (33% more efficient in energy-to-GDP terms since 1995, alongside 50% more total energy used in that same period) as live evidence that efficiency gains have driven higher consumption, not lower, a real-world instance of Jevons Paradox applied at civilizational scale. The Bank of Japan owning over 50% of its own government's bonds is used as a single, concrete, real data point standing in for the much larger abstract claim that debt issuance lets economies keep growing on paper even as real net energy declines — Nate is candid about revising his own prior thinking here, directly naming a 'logical mistake I made a decade or so ago' in assuming GDP would track net energy decline directly, corrected once he accounted for debt's own real capacity to paper over that gap. Closes by explicitly naming the Great Simplification as the real alternative to a Mordor economy, framing this whole show's actual purpose as changing the 'initial conditions' that determine which path a society ends up on, rather than treating either as fixed or inevitable."@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "CORRECTED 2026-08-30: an earlier draft of this stub claimed the episode title's use of 'Mordor' was 'structurally unrelated' to this graph's own ScenarioFacet.Mordor, calling it 'a coincidental echo... not a citation relationship' — this was wrong, based only on a title-level guess before real content was available. The real transcript and show notes confirm this episode is the direct, original explanation of the Mordor economy concept the ScenarioFacet is itself named after — dcterms:references added on ScenarioFacet.Mordor accordingly; see that individual's own scopeNote. Real, further citable material found but NOT built in this pass: Tim Garrett (real energy/thermodynamics researcher, directly cited, not yet a Persona in this graph); the 2021 Earth Day talk (real, distinct prior airing of this same material, external to the numbered TGS/Frankly/RR series so out of this class's scope); episode #37 (Martin Scheringer, PFAS pollution) — already a real, deferred citation elsewhere in this graph (RR-15's own scopeNote), independently reconfirmed here."@en ;
    .

tgs:Monologue.Frankly_108_TheInfluenceOfPsychopaths a thinkr:Episode, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.Monologue ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.Frankly ;
    skos:prefLabel "The Influence of Psychopaths: Why Humans Are Better Than We Think"@en ;
    skos:definition "BUILT OUT 2026-08-30 from a stub, using a complete real transcript and show notes. Real title resolves a discrepancy flagged during the Frankly_126 build — the full official title combines both partial titles found elsewhere in this graph. Directly triggered by two real, recent conversations Nate names explicitly at the open: Luke Kemp's own episode and RR-19 with Reid Meloy and Nancy McWilliams (already built in this graph). Develops a real, structured thesis: aggregate human behavior can be understood via the statistical gap between the median (the typical, still broadly reciprocal person) and the mean (pulled upward/outward by a real, small, disproportionately influential minority — roughly 1% of adult males, per real clinical psychopathy prevalence data cited from the prior week's RR-19 conversation). Real, concrete illustration via David Sloan Wilson's own 'superchicken' experiment (his real, prior TGS appearance, episode #56): selecting hens purely for individual egg output produced aggressive, lower-yield groups, while grouping cooperative hens raised total output — offered as a direct, vivid analogy for the difference between optimizing at the individual versus group level in human institutions."@en ;
    dct:created "2025-09-22"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2025-09-26"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior, tgs:Subject.SystemsScience ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/frankly-original/108-the-influence-of-psychopaths> ;
    thinkr:hasReplay <https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=90ePY2MQ3-Q> ;
    thinkr:hasTranscript <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/09/Frankly-108-Transcript-The-Influence-of-Psychopaths.docx.pdf> ;
    thinkr:discusses tgs:Concept.Superorganism, tgs:Concept.DarkTriad ;
    dct:references tgs:PanelDiscussion.RR_19_DarkTriad ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "Real, direct intellectual debt acknowledged throughout rather than absorbed silently: Nate credits Luke Kemp specifically with a real correction to his own prior thinking on the Superorganism's origin — it wasn't just storable agricultural surplus that mattered, but surplus that could be looted, which is what actually made a hawkish, exploitative strategy pay off and gave rise to real hierarchy. The episode's real analytical core is a structured, six-part account of what happens when a single psychopathic actor enters a group: conditional cooperation unravels as cheating goes visibly unpunished, trust collapses into costly surveillance and contracts (citing Elinor Ostrom's real Nobel-winning commons research on monitoring and sanctioning), norms drift via a real 'bad apple' dynamic, misconduct spreads by visible example, harm intensifies sharply if the actor gains real authority (the 'toxic triangle' of destructive leadership), and finally 'punisher fatigue' sets in as the few people willing to confront bad behavior burn out from insufficient backing. Two real, concrete personal anecdotes ground the more abstract argument: a stranger flashing their headlights to warn Nate his brights were on, and a Helsinki restaurant employee tracking him down through a shopping mall to return a lost wallet containing his passport and cash — offered as real, lived evidence that baseline human reciprocity remains intact beneath the aggregate headlines. The closing register is notably candid about its own incompleteness: Nate explicitly defers concrete solutions to a future episode, offering only two provisional directions — 'select at the right level' (reward group/team performance over individual wins, matching the superchicken analogy directly) and redirecting inherent status-seeking toward pro-social ends rather than trying to eliminate it. Closes with real, disarming self-awareness: 'I started out as a peak oil guy, and now I'm talking about psychopathy and chickens.'"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Real, further citable material found but NOT built in this pass: Luke Kemp's own real two TGS episodes (#153 and #194) — already flagged as a repeat-guest coverage gap in tgs-digest's docs/repeat-guest-coverage.md, independently reconfirmed here with the same two numbers found via Frankly_126. David Sloan Wilson's real episode #56 — previously flagged as deferred across multiple episodes this session (RR-19's own scopeNote among them) — now has a real, confirmed citable episode number, worth building given how many real, independent citations already point to him. Elinor Ostrom (Governing the Commons) and multi-level selection theory (Wilson's own real academic framework) are both directly, substantively discussed but not minted as a Work/Concept in this pass — no existing Concept.MultilevelSelection individual in this graph yet to link to."@en ;
    .

tgs:Interview.TGS_191_TaylorGuthrie a thinkr:Episode, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.Interview ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.MainShow ;
    skos:prefLabel "How Do You Become Who You Want to Be?: The Science Behind Identity, Purpose, and Motivation"@en ;
    skos:definition "BUILT OUT 2026-08-30 from a stub, using a complete real transcript and show notes. Nate hosts social cognitive neuroscientist Taylor Guthrie (University of Oregon PhD, Distinguished Teaching Award) for a real, technical deep dive into how the brain constructs a sense of self — grounded throughout in the ventromedial prefrontal cortex's real, replicated activation pattern whenever a person processes self-relevant information (traits describing oneself, ownership, closeness to others). Develops a real, direct distinction between extrinsic identity (built from external validation, performing a role for recognition) and intrinsic identity (built from genuine reflective alignment), applying this framework to addiction recovery via colleague Elliot Berkman's real 'identity-value model' — successful behavior change tends to come from adopting an identity ('I'm a healthy person') rather than pursuing a discrete goal ('lose 20 pounds'). Already cited by two other episodes in this graph (RR-26 and Frankly_143) as this graph's own primary source for the Default Mode Network — confirmed directly here, not inferred."@en ;
    dct:created "2025-06-12"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2025-08-20"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    thinkr:hasGuest tgs:Persona.TaylorGuthrie ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/episode/191-taylor-guthrie> ;
    thinkr:hasReplay <https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=_lBKrPvDJo8> ;
    thinkr:hasTranscript <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/08/TGS-191-Taylor-Guthrie-Transcript.pdf> ;
    thinkr:discusses tgs:Concept.DefaultModeNetwork ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "A real, substantive philosophical disagreement structures the episode's most pointed section: Guthrie directly names his own real tension with Robert Sapolsky's hard determinism (Nate's own prior guest, already built in this graph), while still crediting Sapolsky's core claim — the present moment is genuinely, heavily constrained by one's full personal and evolutionary history. Guthrie's own resolution draws on Viktor Frankl's real 'space between stimulus and response' framing (which he notes Nate has himself invoked on other episodes) to argue this isn't the same as denying will entirely: 'it's not free will, but it's still will.' The real, concrete center of the conversation is attention itself — Guthrie's own direct claim that humans are consciously aware of roughly 2% of what the brain actually processes at any moment, used to explain the real, common experience of knowing you value nature while still reflexively reaching for a phone mid-hike, reframed not as hypocrisy but as real-time resource allocation under fluctuating internal states. The episode's most personally revealing moment comes in its final minutes: Guthrie discloses, largely unprompted, that becoming a father in the weeks before this recording directly reshaped his relationship to pessimistic futures content he'd previously consumed 'voraciously' — finding it newly difficult to sit with doom-oriented material while actively raising children he wants to see reach their full potential. His own real, concrete magic-wand answer is notably specific rather than abstract: eliminating short-form video content (TikTok, Instagram Reels) specifically, grounded in his own real classroom practice — a three-week 'go without' addiction assignment where giving up short-form video reliably proves harder for students than expected, revealing how powerfully these platforms have colonized attention. Closes on a real, direct piece of advice distinct from the episode's neuroscience framing: 'choose your groups wisely,' arguing that much of what feels like intrinsically-held identity is in fact shaped by which relationships and communities a person has embedded themselves in."@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Real, further citable material found but NOT built in this pass: Elliot Berkman (Guthrie's own real colleague, cited directly for the identity-value model of addiction/behavior change); Dan McAdams (real narrative-identity researcher, cited directly for his 'redemptive arc vs. contamination' framework of trauma response); Slavoj Žižek (real philosopher, cited — though not by name in the excerpt reviewed — for his framing of ideology as defining 'what you're blind to being able to believe'). This episode is now confirmed, not just cross-referenced, as this graph's own primary real source on the Default Mode Network, matching what both RR-26 and Frankly_143 had already asserted about it before this build existed."@en ;
    .

