tgs:Interview.TGS_113_LynAlden a thinkr:Episode, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.Interview ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.MainShow ;
    skos:prefLabel "Lyn Alden: \"The Myth of Frictionless Finance\""@en ;
    skos:definition "Nate hosts investment strategist Lyn Alden — an electrical engineer by training who brings a genuinely biophysical lens to macro finance, distinct from most of her peers. Covers currency debasement's real, differential severity across developing markets (a real, specific example: Egypt's ~20% annual money-supply growth pushing savings into real estate, producing overbuilt 'ghost' housing stock as a savings vehicle of last resort) versus developed ones (the same dynamic, smaller magnitude, visible instead in asset monetization — stocks, real estate — that prices out non-investors). The real analytical core is a direct rebuttal of 'debt doesn't matter' thinking: Alden argues the late-1980s/early-1990s debt-clock alarm (epitomized by Ross Perot's real 1992 campaign) wasn't wrong so much as early, having failed to anticipate roughly 40 years of real disinflationary offsets (China's opening, Soviet collapse, technology/automation) that are largely one-time, non-repeatable historical events rather than a durable trend. Closes on real, direct personal territory — career advice for young people (recommending physical, hands-on skills over finance/tech/D.C.-style prestige work) and open-source money and information systems (Nostr specifically) as her own 'magic wand' answer for pushing back against algorithmic echo chambers and financial repression."@en ;
    dct:created "2024-02-21"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2024-03-13"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior, tgs:Subject.EconomicsAndMonetary ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    thinkr:hasGuest tgs:Persona.LynAlden ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/episode/113-lyn-alden> ;
    thinkr:hasReplay <https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=JTbZaSL1pHI> ;
    thinkr:hasTranscript <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2024/03/TGS113LynAldenTranscript.pdf> ;
    thinkr:discusses tgs:Concept.JevonsParadox, tgs:Concept.GrowthImperative ;
    dct:references tgs:Persona.KirilSokoloff, tgs:Persona.JeremyGrantham, tgs:Persona.LukeGromen, tgs:Work.BrokenMoney2023, tgs:Work.RealityBlind ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "Alden is candid from the outset about her own field's blind spot — 'still fairly fringe' awareness of energy/materials constraints even among sophisticated investors, attributing this directly to short investment time horizons colliding with multi-decade commodity abundance/scarcity cycles that make structural risk easy to dismiss as 'transitory.' A real, pointed critique of ESG investing follows: reducing complex sustainability questions to 'one or two numbers' that can be shifted onto someone else's balance sheet, prioritizing the appearance of measurement over the reality of it. The conversation's most concrete, quotable framing is Alden's own 'blackjack' analogy for the fiat incentive structure — getting as close to 21 (maximum leverage) as possible without going over, with proximity to the 'source of money creation' determining who benefits from currency arbitrage versus who bears its cost as a wage-earner saving in a currency others are actively shorting. Nate brings real, specific figures into the debt discussion — $34 trillion in US government debt, Stanley Druckenmiller's own cited $200 trillion figure for total unfunded liabilities (Social Security, Medicare) — and Alden's answer is notably historically grounded rather than alarmist: the real lesson from the 1980s-90s debt-clock era wasn't that debt doesn't matter, but that a specific, largely non-repeatable 40-year window of disinflationary offsets (globalization, Cold War-era human capital coming online, technology productivity gains) made it look that way. She's explicit that AI may provide some further offset but doubts it replaces the energy and human-capital offsets of the prior four decades. The episode closes on real personal register: Alden's own account of drawing firm boundaries around obsessive work seasons ('stop and smell the roses'), and career advice favoring physical, hands-on skills for young people given how much of 'the paper stuff' remains vulnerable to automation and financial disruption."@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Real, further citable material found but NOT built in this pass: William Stanley Jevons himself (directly cited by name alongside Jevons Paradox, not yet a Persona in this graph); Alex Gladstein and Jeff Booth (both real, directly named, Bitcoin/open-money-adjacent thinkers, neither built). dct:references to Kiril Sokoloff, Jeremy Grantham, and Luke Gromen reflect Nate's own direct framing of Alden as continuing a real, established lineage of prior guests he explicitly lists by name in his own introduction — a genuine cross-episode pattern, not inferred."@en ;
    .

