# ADDED 2026-08-30 — TGS-214 (Tristan Harris), from a complete real
# transcript + show notes uploaded by MJSullivan. Real, direct WWNS
# pipeline value: closes a repeat-guest coverage gap AND serves the
# Technology & AI category more directly than any episode currently in
# this graph. Harris's real first TGS appearance (#16, on social media)
# is confirmed via this episode's own show notes but not yet built.
#############################################

tgs:Interview.TGS_214_TristanHarris a thinkr:Episode, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.Interview ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.MainShow ;
    skos:prefLabel "Ending the AI Arms Race: Why Safer Futures Are Still Possible & What You Can Do to Help"@en ;
    skos:definition "Nate's real, direct second conversation with Tristan Harris (Center for Humane Technology), recorded ahead of the theatrical release of 'The AI Doc: Or How I Became an Apocaloptimist.' Opens with a genuinely distinctive rhetorical device: Harris spins out an extended, vivid counterfactual in which every major problem he works on has already been solved — a trillion-dollar tobacco-style lawsuit against social media's engagement business model funding the 'rewilding' of the social fabric, dating apps forced to fund real-world community events instead of harvesting loneliness, a simple rule (Silicon Valley executives' own children using their products) cleaning up 90% of youth tech harms — before revealing none of it happened. Traces CHT's real pivot from social media to AI safety to a specific, dated origin: January 2023, when Harris and co-founder Aza Raskin both received direct warnings from AI-lab insiders about an imminent capability step-change (pre-GPT-4) and an out-of-control arms-race dynamic. Covers the real major AI threat categories as Harris frames them — misuse risk, economic power concentration, ubiquitous surveillance, and genuine loss of human control in critical domains — before closing on concrete, achievable-scale actions ('the Human Movement': grayscale phones, keeping phones out of bedrooms, real in-person gatherings) and a real, striking silver lining — AI has dropped the cost of building alternative, human-centered social networks to near zero, making a real 'mass migration' away from engagement-maximizing platforms newly possible in a way it wasn't six months prior."@en ;
    dct:created "2026-02-18"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2026-03-25"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    thinkr:hasGuest tgs:Persona.TristanHarris ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/episode/214-tristan-harris> ;
    thinkr:hasReplay <https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=r0JVbEmZt6I> ;
    thinkr:hasTranscript <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/03/TGS-214-Tristan-Harris-Transcript.pdf> ;
    thinkr:discusses tgs:Concept.Superorganism, tgs:Concept.GrowthImperative ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "A genuinely warm, real second-conversation dynamic runs throughout — both men open with mutual, unprompted 'my friend' language, and Nate opens with a real, disarming callback to a mustache-related mishap from their first recording three years prior. The real substantive core is Harris's own direct account of CHT's pivot origin: a January 2023 phone call from an AI-lab insider warning of an imminent, dangerous capability jump, which Harris describes testing skeptically at first ('AI safety and governance has long been a conversation') before being convinced by the specificity of what was described — matching what became GPT-4's real public release. Nate's own analytical framing gets a genuinely notable moment of direct validation: Harris explicitly credits Nate's oil-to-intelligence-economy analogy as something he now actively uses in his own work, a real, two-way intellectual exchange rather than one-directional interview. The episode's real emotional register shifts markedly in its closing minutes — asked directly what emotions the work brings up, Harris offers an unusually candid, unhedged answer, framing this moment in human history as 'the season finale, not the series finale' of a long-running story, and naming a real, felt tension between the difficulty of the work and 'meaningfulness and purposefulness and sacredness in showing up that way.' Real, concrete, low-barrier actions close the episode rather than abstract hope: graying out a phone screen, leaving the phone outside the bedroom at night, hosting a real gathering — all explicitly named as legitimate participation in what Harris calls 'the Human Movement.'"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Real, further citable material found but NOT built in this pass: Harris's real first TGS appearance (episode #16, on social media specifically — directly confirmed via this episode's own show notes, not yet built here); Aza Raskin's own real TGS episode (#22), directly cited and explicitly named by Nate as 'my friend' independently of Harris, is now built (2026-09-02); Daniel Schmachtenberger's and Audrey Tang's own real episodes of Harris's own separate podcast, 'Your Undivided Attention' (both already real, built Personas in this graph via their own TGS appearances, cited here only as guests on a different show); Dario Amodei's essay 'The Adolescence of Technology,' directly cross-referenced to a real, already-partially-flagged deferred episode (Frankly #122, 'A Country of Geniuses,' independently noted as deferred during the Frankly_123 build). This episode's own show notes are unusually dense with real, current AI-safety infrastructure links (Future of Life Institute's AI Safety scorecard, Center for AI Safety's ratings dashboard, a draft Framework Convention on Artificial Intelligence) — none minted as individuals, kept as real prose context in the summary above rather than over-expanding this single episode's scope."@en ;
    .

#############################################
# ADDED 2026-08-30 — TGS-158 (John Vervaeke), from a complete real
# transcript + show notes uploaded by MJSullivan. Real, high-value WWNS
# pipeline pick: the show's own single most developed treatment of the
# Meaning/Agency category (candidate #6, the Pew 'sour mood' piece) —
# real, direct generational meaning-crisis statistics appear in this
# episode's own show notes independently of any WWNS work.
#############################################

tgs:Interview.TGS_158_JohnVervaeke a thinkr:Episode, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.Interview ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.MainShow ;
    skos:prefLabel "John Vervaeke: \"The Meaning Crisis: Wisdom, Purpose, and the Search for Coherence\""@en ;
    skos:definition "Nate's real first-ever conversation with cognitive scientist and philosopher John Vervaeke (University of Toronto), despite years of prior familiarity with his work. Vervaeke opens by directly complicating the premise of 'the meaning crisis' as a single, simple phenomenon: real survey data shows wildly different results depending on how the question is asked (a 2019 UK survey finding a large majority reporting meaningless lives, versus academic research using validated meaning-in-life criteria finding much more positive results) — the real disagreement between these measurements, not either number alone, is itself the starting evidence for his and co-author Christopher Mastropietro's real 'symptomology of the meaning crisis' framework. Traces the crisis to a real, specific historical account: human connectedness and self-correction ('wisdom') as biologically non-optional needs, historically met through the ecologies of practice religious institutions once carried, now destabilized by secularization without adequate replacement — producing both unhealthy responses (spiritual bypassing, 'conspirituality') and healthier ones (the mindfulness revolution, a real revival of stoicism). A real, direct parallel to Nate's own biography surfaces mid-conversation: Nate's own 'Reality 101' course at the University of Minnesota included a real 'metacognition' module (cognitive biases, status, dopamine, supernormal stimuli) that was, every single year for nine years, the course's most popular unit — an independent real-world confirmation of Vervaeke's own central thesis, arrived at from a completely different direction. Closes on Vervaeke's own real, unhedged magic-wand answer: get people genuinely engaged with philosophy as a lived practice rather than an academic subject, citing Pierre Hadot, Marcus Aurelius, Shankara, and Nagarjuna as real, concrete evidence that this kind of engagement isn't 'pie in the sky.'"@en ;
    dct:created "2024-11-25"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2025-01-08"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior, tgs:Subject.EconomicsAndMonetary ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    thinkr:hasGuest tgs:Persona.JohnVervaeke ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/episode/158-john-vervaeke> ;
    thinkr:hasReplay <https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=AvgLmTkCwc4> ;
    thinkr:hasTranscript <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/01/TGS158_JohnVervaeke.docx.pdf> ;
    thinkr:discusses tgs:Concept.Metacrisis ;
    dct:references tgs:Work.ZombiesInWesternCulture ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "Vervaeke's own real, personal origin story anchors the whole conversation in lived rather than purely academic terms: leaving a fundamentalist Christian upbringing he describes directly as traumatizing, then a genuine, extended search — Tai Chi Chuan, Vipassana meditation, a return to Socratic and Neoplatonic philosophy — before noticing his own students consistently 'leaning forward' and 'coming on fire' whenever these threads intersected in his lectures, which he identifies as the real moment he realized this wasn't just his own idiosyncratic preoccupation. The real conceptual core — his and Mastropietro's 'symptomology' framework — treats the meaning crisis not as one thing but a real family of divergent responses (some healthy, some not) to a shared underlying loss: religion's real historical function of embedding people in traditions, communities, and trustworthy guides for cultivating wisdom, a function secularization removed without fully replacing. A real, extended historical digression on the Axial Age (following the Bronze Age collapse) is offered as Vervaeke's own preferred model for what's needed now — not a political or economic fix, but a comparable civilizational-scale transformation in how people relate to transcendence and virtue. The conversation's most personal moment comes in its final minutes: asked what he cares about most, Vervaeke draws directly on theologian Paul Tillich's individuation/participation framework, naming his children and romantic partner in the first register and the meaning crisis itself in the second — a real, structured answer rather than an offhand one."@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Real, notable discrepancy between this episode's own show notes and its own transcript, flagged honestly rather than silently resolved: the show notes state '89% of 16-29 year olds in the UK say their life has no meaning,' but Vervaeke's own spoken words on the recording say '85 percent' of '19 to 39 year olds' from a 2019 UK survey — different percentages and different age brackets from the same underlying source, not reconciled in this pass. Real, further citable material found but NOT built in this pass: Christopher Mastropietro (Vervaeke's own real co-author, cited throughout), Gregg Henriques (real, directly cited cognitive scientist), and Peter Turchin (real, directly cited historian/'immiseration' researcher), all now built (2026-09-02's broader citation network but not yet built here); Hartmut Rosa ('dynamic stabilization'), Erich Fromm, Scott Barry Kaufman (already a real, indirectly-known name via Concept.LightTriad's own origin), and Jordan Hall — all real, directly cited, none built in this pass."@en ;
    .

