# ADDED 2026-08-30 — TGS-98 (Helen Thompson), from a complete real
# transcript + show notes uploaded by MJSullivan. Closes one of the real
# repeat-guest coverage gaps (docs/repeat-guest-coverage.md, tgs-digest).
# Real, already-resolved data-quality note: this transcript's own source
# file was originally misnamed as episode #97 (which the site's real URL
# actually maps to Chuck Watson, already a built Persona via RR-21) —
# the site itself resolves this specific content to #98, matching what
# was already recorded in this graph's own repeat-guest tracker before
# this build. Trusted the site's real resolution, not the stale filename.
#############################################

tgs:Interview.TGS_98_HelenThompson a thinkr:Episode, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.Interview ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.MainShow ;
    skos:prefLabel "Helen Thompson: \"The Complex History of Energy and Geopolitics\""@en ;
    skos:definition "Nate's real first conversation with Cambridge political economist Helen Thompson, whose own book, tgs:Work.Disorder2022, Nate directly calls 'a political science mirror twin of the logic of The Great Simplification.' Thompson traces the deep, real history connecting fossil fuel geopolitics to major 20th and 21st century political shocks: the 1956 Suez Crisis (Britain acting as the West's designated Middle East oil-security guarantor, until Eisenhower's real financial pressure ended that arrangement), the Iranian Revolution's real effect on Israel's oil-supply relationships, and the Nixon Shock and Volcker Shock's role in cementing the petrodollar system. She's direct about a real, striking irony in her own field: political economy was relatively energy-aware in the 1970s, lost that awareness sometime in the late 1980s or 1990s (roughly tracking Western politicians' own loss of energy awareness), and has only recently begun regaining it."@en ;
    dct:created "2023-10-30"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2023-11-15"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior, tgs:Subject.EconomicsAndMonetary ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    thinkr:hasGuest tgs:Persona.HelenThompson ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/episode/98-helen-thompson> ;
    thinkr:hasReplay <https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=FQbdNXQcT3E> ;
    thinkr:hasTranscript <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2023/11/TGS97HelenThompsonTranscript.docx.pdf> ;
    dct:references tgs:Work.Disorder2022, tgs:Work.OilAndTheWesternEconomicCrisis, tgs:Interview.TGS_54_TheStealthAdjustmentOfOil ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "A real, vivid personal origin grounds an otherwise densely historical conversation: Thompson's own first memory of national political life is the winter of 1974, age seven, watching her mother cook by candlelight during Britain's real widespread power cuts — a formative, visceral encounter with energy scarcity she carried for decades before consciously connecting it to her scholarship around 2013, via the post-2008 monetary environment's real links to quantitative easing, zero interest rates, and the US shale boom. A genuinely striking real coincidence surfaces early: the UK edition of her book Disorder released on the exact day Russia invaded Ukraine, which she describes candidly reshaping public reception of arguments — her own real chapter on the Suez Crisis, written with real hesitation ('this could go very wrong... people would think it's some kind of apology for British imperialism'), suddenly became the single most-asked-about section once readers connected 1956's transit and pipeline politics to the unfolding Ukraine conflict. Her own real, direct research-agenda proposal in the closing section is notably concrete: a real, still-unwritten project tracing the relationship between monetary history and energy history across the entire 20th century, back to the 1870s origins of the gold standard — offered as a genuine open question for the 'incredible and underutilized' population of postdocs Nate raises directly. Thompson's own real magic-wand answer is unusually self-questioning rather than a simple wish: she first considers fusion energy, then directly interrogates her own answer — 'nobody's got any idea what it would mean to be a human being' if energy were fully unconstrained, since fossil-fuel scarcity itself has driven much historical conflict — before settling on higher collective self-awareness and a less materially-selfish consciousness as her real, considered answer instead."@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Real, further citable material found but NOT built in this pass: the historical/geopolitical sweep across this episode is exceptionally dense (Suez Crisis, Iranian Revolution, Abraham Accords, petrodollar system, Nixon Shock, Volcker Shock, Ukraine/Crimea) — kept as prose in the discussion summary above rather than minted as individual Concept/Work entries, since none of it constitutes a single citable external source the way a paper or book does. Art Berman's real TGS episodes are directly cited (already built, TGS_54); Vladimir Putin's own real energy-focused doctoral thesis is directly cited but not modeled as a Work."@en ;
    .

#############################################
# ADDED 2026-08-30 — TGS-215 (Chris Keefer, real second appearance),
# from a complete real transcript + show notes uploaded by MJSullivan.
# Closes the last-but-one real repeat-guest coverage gap
# (docs/repeat-guest-coverage.md, tgs-digest). Real, current-events
# episode: recorded live during the real March 2026 Iran war, with the
# original planned nuclear-energy topic reframed around it in real time.
# His real first appearance (TGS_123, "Empowering the Future: from
# Nuclear to Podcasting") is confirmed but not yet built.
#############################################

tgs:Interview.TGS_215_ChrisKeefer a thinkr:Episode, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.Interview ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.MainShow ;
    skos:prefLabel "Scrambling for Energy Security: Navigating Unstable Energy Supplies Amidst Global Conflict"@en ;
    skos:definition "Nate's real second conversation with emergency physician and nuclear-energy advocate Chris Keefer (Decouple podcast, Canadians for Nuclear Energy), recorded live the weekend of a real, major escalation: US/Israeli strikes on one of Iran's largest natural gas fields, and Iran's retaliatory strike on Qatar's. Nate directly timestamps the recording — Sunday morning, March 22nd — noting the conversation's originally planned focus (general nuclear energy) was reframed in real time by unfolding events. Keefer grounds the moment in a direct historical parallel: the 1970s energy shocks, which drove real, divergent national responses still shaping today's energy landscape — France's real rapid nuclear buildout (37 of 57 reactors built in the 1970s), the UK and Norway's North Sea oil expansion, West Germany's real Cold War-era gas diplomacy with the Soviet Union, and the US's real shift from oil-fired to coal-fired electricity."@en ;
    dct:created "2026-03-22"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2026-04-01"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.EnergySystems, tgs:Subject.Geopolitics ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    thinkr:hasGuest tgs:Persona.ChrisKeefer ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/episode/215-chris-keefer> ;
    thinkr:hasReplay <https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=wFwQBknfWeg> ;
    thinkr:hasTranscript <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/03/TGS-215-Chris-Keefer-Transcript-1.pdf> ;
    dct:references tgs:Interview.TGS_127_EdConway ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "Keefer opens with a direct, real application of Maslow's hierarchy to macro energy politics: the 2010s-2020s were spent, in his words, 'at the lofty heights' of the pyramid — cheap capital, abundant energy, room for higher-order political and cultural debate — while acute energy crises reliably 'rub our faces down into the foundations,' physiological needs like heat, water, and security. His real, central technical argument is a direct rebuttal of small modular and micro-reactor enthusiasm, delivered through concrete unit economics: a real, worked example shows a 10-megawatt microreactor generating roughly $13 million a year at $130/MWh — nowhere near enough to cover capital costs, operators, and security once actually priced out, versus proven, large gigawatt-scale reactors, the only design that has 'proven itself economic' at real scale historically (citing the US Army's own real, failed microreactor experiments in Cold War-era Greenland and the Panama Canal as direct historical precedent). The conversation's most genuinely candid moment comes when Nate asks what's changed in Keefer's worldview since their first conversation two years earlier: Keefer discloses going 'from being an idealist to a realist,' still fully committed to nuclear advocacy but explicitly scaling back the scope of what he believes it — or he — can single-handedly change, framing this recalibration as psychologically healthier despite the reduced ambition. The closing register turns genuinely personal and philosophical: Keefer, drawing directly on his own medical background in palliative care, describes deliberately living as though a real, specific AI-extinction-risk timeline (three years, a figure he calls 'crazy' even as he takes it seriously) were true — not from despair, but because confronting mortality directly has sharpened how he spends time with his seven-year-old son, picking him up from school daily rather than after-care. He names his own psychological style directly, tying a real, self-diagnosed 'defensive pessimism' to his Ukrainian heritage and its long history of great-power conflict."@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Real, further citable material found but NOT built in this pass: Luke Kemp's real first TGS episode (#153, directly cited here; his second appearance, #194, is already built in this graph — dct:references deliberately not asserted to #153 since it doesn't exist as an individual yet); Sheldon Solomon (real TGS episode #199, directly cited for Terror Management Theory), now built (2026-09-02). Nate Soares, also directly cited here, is now built (2026-09-02). Nate's own real academic paper, 'Economics for the future — Beyond the superorganism,' is directly cited again here (independently confirmed via multiple other episodes this session) — still not modeled as its own Work individual, consistent with established practice."@en ;
    .

