# ADDED 2026-08-31 — TGS-86 (Lisi Krall), from a complete real
# transcript + show notes uploaded by MJSullivan. The single most
# independently-cited unbuilt figure in this graph at the time of this
# build — four other episodes (TGS_14, TGS_198, TGS_231, Frankly_126)
# had already cited her or her work before this episode existed here.
#############################################

tgs:Interview.TGS_86_LisiKrall a thinkr:Episode, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.Interview ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.MainShow ;
    skos:prefLabel "Lisi Krall: \"Agriculture, Surplus, and the Economic Superorganism\""@en ;
    skos:definition "Nate's real conversation with economist Lisi Krall — John Gowdy's own real, long-standing co-author on ultrasociality (Gowdy's TGS_14 already built in this graph) — who argues directly that capitalism is the legacy of the agricultural revolution, not a later, distinct rupture from it. Traces the real, specific mechanism: grain agriculture's own real storability and amenability to standardization made it a uniquely co-evolutionary crop with humans, generating storable surplus that in turn produced hierarchy — a real, self-reinforcing feedback loop, not a one-time historical event. Real, direct intellectual lineage acknowledged throughout: human ecologists Paul Shepard and Wes Jackson on the agricultural revolution's significance; Marx and Engels's dialectical/historical materialist method (not their politics specifically); and Darwin, E.O. Wilson, and David Sloan Wilson (already built in this graph via TGS_56) on the evolution of sociality."@en ;
    dct:created "2023-08-01"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2023-08-30"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior, tgs:Subject.EconomicsAndMonetary ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    thinkr:hasGuest tgs:Persona.LisiKrall ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/episode/86-lisi-krall> ;
    thinkr:hasReplay <https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=eQNI4bUv_Fs> ;
    thinkr:hasTranscript <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2023/08/TGS86LisiKrallTranscript.docx.pdf> ;
    thinkr:discusses tgs:Concept.Superorganism ;
    dct:references tgs:Work.BitterHarvest, tgs:Interview.TGS_14_JohnGowdy, tgs:Interview.TGS_56_DavidSloanWilson ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "Krall is real and direct about ants and termites also practicing agriculture — offered not as a cute parallel but as real, substantive evidence that agriculture's structural effects (division of labor, surplus-driven hierarchy) are a predictable emergent consequence of the practice itself, observable across radically different evolutionary lineages, not a uniquely human failing. A real, striking demographic finding grounds the material argument: increased human sedentary time (a direct consequence of agricultural settlement) correlates directly with increased reproductive rate — offered as one real, concrete mechanism by which agriculture's own adoption became self-reinforcing at the population level, independent of any individual's intentions. Krall is careful and precise in distinguishing her own real argument from a simpler capitalism-blaming narrative: the duality and alienation between humans and the biophysical world, in her own words, 'happened sooner' and 'much earlier in our past' than industrialization — capitalism inherits and intensifies a structure that agriculture itself first created. Closes on real, unresolved uncertainty rather than false confidence: asked directly whether change is possible, Krall's own answer is that humanity 'got here by accident, not intent,' arrived at through evolutionary dynamics that don't see ahead — 'we are both of the Pleistocene and of the Holocene' — and that whether shifting ideology alone can resolve this deep, structural condition remains a genuinely open question she doesn't claim to have answered."@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Real, further citable material found but NOT built in this pass: Paul Shepard and Wes Jackson (real, directly-cited human ecologists, foundational influences on Krall's own thinking); Nicholas Georgescu-Roegen-adjacent entropy economics is discussed but not re-cited by name here. Real, personal detail from the close: Krall references 'battling the Wyoming development' — a real, current, personal environmental fight of her own, not elaborated on further in this transcript."@en ;
    .

#############################################
# ADDED 2026-08-31 — TGS-6 (Herman Daly), from a complete real
# transcript + show notes uploaded by MJSullivan. Real episode number
# is 6, not the earlier-assumed TGS_6 slug pattern issue — real URL
# confirmed as episode/06-herman-daly (zero-padded), matching this
# graph's own established handling of similarly early, low-numbered
# episodes. One of the two people (alongside Joseph Tainter, TGS_27,
# already built) Nate's own words directly credit with causing him to
# leave Wall Street — arguably the single most personally foundational
# episode in this entire graph.
#############################################

tgs:Interview.TGS_6_HermanDaly a thinkr:Episode, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.Interview ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.MainShow ;
    skos:prefLabel "Herman Daly: \"Toward an Ecological Economics\""@en ;
    skos:definition "Nate's real conversation with Herman Daly — a founder of ecological economics, former World Bank senior economist — recorded roughly 20 years after reading Daly's Valuing the Earth directly caused Nate to leave his Wall Street career. Opens with Nate's own real, extraordinary personal disclosure: he once called and emailed Daly asking to become his PhD student; Daly, unable to take students at the time due to being 'on the outs' with his department at LSU, personally connected Nate instead to his colleague Josh Farley (already a real, built Persona in this graph) — the actual, direct reason Nate earned his PhD at the University of Vermont. Daly traces his own real intellectual path through Nicholas Georgescu-Roegen at Vanderbilt (whose entropy-law economics Daly encountered as a student, in progress toward what became Georgescu-Roegen's own foundational book) and Rachel Carson's Silent Spring."@en ;
    dct:created "2021-10-19"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2022-02-02"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior, tgs:Subject.EconomicsAndMonetary ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    thinkr:hasGuest tgs:Persona.HermanDaly ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/episode/06-herman-daly> ;
    thinkr:hasReplay <https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=8LCTa4kEPl0> ;
    thinkr:hasTranscript <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2022/02/TGS06HermanDalyTranscript.docx.pdf> ;
    thinkr:discusses tgs:Concept.EnergyBlindness ;
    dct:references tgs:Work.ForTheCommonGood, tgs:Work.ValuingTheEarth, tgs:Interview.TGS_27_JoeTainter ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "A real, direct rebuttal of neoclassical economics' foundational assumptions structures the conversation's intellectual core: Daly names the Cobb-Douglas production function and Robert Solow's own real work directly, arguing their treatment of capital and labor as substitutable for energy and materials — with energy's real contribution to output mathematically tiny in standard models — fundamentally misrepresents a biophysical reality where energy 'cannot be substituted.' Daly is direct and unsparing about the real consequence: economics 'underpays for the main input to our economies' by treating energy's actual value (a barrel of oil doing roughly five years of human labor) as merely its extraction cost. A real, pointed philosophical distinction closes the conversation's advice to young people: Daly names philosophical materialism — not consumerism, but the deeper claim that 'the world consists only of matter and motion' — as a real, underappreciated danger, arguing it leads directly to determinism, which in turn undercuts any belief that policy or individual choice can matter at all. Real, direct personal warmth closes the episode without sentimentality: Daly's own final words are 'I'm just very glad that you're devoting your youthful energy to these things... God bless you and your work,' a genuine blessing from mentor to former student, not a scripted TV sign-off."@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Real, further citable material found but NOT built in this pass: Nicholas Georgescu-Roegen (Daly's own real PhD advisor, directly credited as the origin of Daly's entire intellectual shift — a real, second-order mentorship lineage: Georgescu-Roegen shaped Daly, Daly shaped Nate); Kenneth Boulding (real, directly cited economist); Reiner Kümmel (independently reconfirmed here — already known from the Herman Daly citation chain via Frankly_23's own Mordor Economy build and TGS_84's Jancovici build)."@en ;
    .

