# ADDED 2026-08-31 — TGS-120 (Tom Chi), from a complete real transcript
# + show notes uploaded by MJSullivan. Independently cited by both the
# Brett KenCairn (TGS_222) and Peter Brannen (TGS_210) builds before
# this episode itself existed here.
#############################################

tgs:Interview.TGS_120_TomChi a thinkr:Episode, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.Interview ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.MainShow ;
    skos:prefLabel "Tom Chi: \"Net Positive for the Planet – from Beavers to Bionics\""@en ;
    skos:definition "Nate hosts physicist-turned-investor Tom Chi (At One Ventures, formerly a founding member of Google X) for a real, structured account of what it would take for the economy to become genuinely net positive to the biosphere, not merely less negative. Chi frames the goal in real, deliberately long timescales — 200 to 500 years for a full transition, since even a best-case scenario would still take roughly 50 years just for the cryosphere to physically re-stabilize once emissions actually stopped. Lays out three real epochs of ecological technology, starting with 'material productivity' (extracting dramatically more real economic value from the same physical feedstock — his own real example: a kilogram of sand worth pennies as construction material versus potentially thousands of dollars as semiconductor-grade silicon) before building toward a fuller net-positive economic redesign."@en ;
    dct:created "2024-03-18"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2024-04-24"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior, tgs:Subject.EconomicsAndMonetary ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    thinkr:hasGuest tgs:Persona.TomChi ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/episode/120-tom-chi> ;
    thinkr:hasReplay <https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=AjGOGfzAvyc> ;
    thinkr:hasTranscript <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2024/04/TGS120TomChiTranscript.pdf> ;
    thinkr:discusses tgs:Concept.JevonsParadox, tgs:Concept.MaximumPowerPrinciple ;
    dct:references tgs:PanelDiscussion.RR_6_FossilFreeFoodSystems ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "A real, direct personal disclosure opens the episode with unusual candor: Nate admits that at a dinner four or five years earlier, his real first impression of Chi was 'this guy doesn't get it,' before a more recent dinner led him to a real, humbling realization — 'maybe I am the one that doesn't get it' — and a hastily scheduled recording. Chi's own real biomimicry examples ground the abstract framework concretely: North America once held over 100 million beavers whose dam-building created real, substantial carbon-sequestering wetland ecosystems now largely gone, and indigenous topsoil depths of 5+ feet across much of the pre-colonial Americas have been reduced to a few inches under industrial agriculture. Real, current portfolio examples anchor Chi's own thesis in practice rather than theory: Gradient (air conditioning without the long-lived fluorinated 'F-gas' refrigerants used industry-wide, some with 10,000-50,000 year atmospheric half-lives) and Dendra Systems (ecological restoration technology). Chi's own real magic-wand answer is conceptual rather than technological: reminding people directly that money was created to track value, not to be value itself — arguing that remembering this distinction would reveal real, systemic mismatches between the two currently obscured by treating money as the actual goal. Closes on real, direct personal warmth — Nate asks Chi to 'come back in six, 12 months' with an update — and Chi's own real, telling detail about his firm's own name: deliberately not named after himself, since 'who wants to go and work at something named after somebody who died like 500 years ago.'"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Real, further citable material found but NOT built in this pass: this episode's own show notes directly cross-reference RR-6's real four panelists (Jason Bradford, Andrew Millison, Vandana Shiva, Daniel Zetah) — all four already real, built Personas in this graph via that episode; no new names surfaced beyond what's already captured."@en ;
    .

