# ADDED 2026-08-31 — TGS-53 (William Rees), from a complete real
# transcript + show notes uploaded by MJSullivan. His solo episode
# number was previously only known via a bare YouTube URL, not a
# confirmed canonical number — now confirmed directly.
#############################################

tgs:Interview.TGS_53_WilliamRees a thinkr:Episode, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.Interview ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.MainShow ;
    skos:prefLabel "William E. Rees: \"The Fundamental Issue – Overshoot\""@en ;
    skos:definition "Nate's real solo conversation with population ecologist William Rees — originator of Ecological Footprint Analysis — tracing a real, formative childhood memory as its own real origin point: age 10, on his grandparents' Ontario farm, realizing mid-meal that everything on his plate he'd had a direct hand in growing, a real, visceral 'we are what we eat' moment he describes as feeling like 'free fall in an elevator.' Rees is direct about his own real, decades-long professional struggle: wanting to be a 'human ecologist' studying humans within ecological systems, but finding no academic department willing to hire him for it in the 1970s — he ultimately spent his entire career in a school of community and regional planning rather than a biology department, simply because it was the one place willing to let him apply ecological thinking to human systems."@en ;
    dct:created "2022-12-06"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2023-01-11"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior, tgs:Subject.EconomicsAndMonetary ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    thinkr:hasGuest tgs:Persona.WilliamRees ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/episode/53-william-rees> ;
    thinkr:hasReplay <https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=LQTuDttP2Yg> ;
    thinkr:hasTranscript <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2023/01/TGS53WilliamReesTranscript.docx.pdf> ;
    thinkr:discusses tgs:Concept.EcologicalFootprint ;
    dct:references tgs:Interview.TGS_7_JoshFarleyAppearance1, tgs:Interview.TGS_29_JoshFarleyAppearance2 ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "Rees's real, central structural critique is that humans have institutionally exempted themselves from ecology: ecologists study non-human species and rarely study humans directly, while economics — 'which really ought to be human ecology' — ignores the actual ecosystems the economy depends on, leaving 'neither economists nor mainstream plant and animal ecologists' equipped to address the real human predicament. A real, striking framing runs throughout: humans are, in real ecological terms, the dominant consumer, producer, and predator species in every major ecosystem type on the planet — a fact Rees argues is simply never taught, in any curriculum he's aware of. His own real, direct account of a public dispute closes the intellectual section: a real, published Nature debate with economist Bjørn Lomborg, cited directly in this episode's own show notes, over whether ecological limits are being overstated. The conversation's real emotional core surfaces in Rees's own answer about what he cares most about — recounting weeping on the ground during Arctic PhD fieldwork at the sheer beauty and productivity of undisturbed ecosystems, and framing humans as literally recycled matter from deep time ('you undoubtedly have thousands of molecules that once roamed the great plains... as Tyrannosaurus Rex'). His own real, direct magic-wand answer is characteristically unhedged for a 'benevolent dictator' question: a new global constitution built around avoiding ecological overshoot, requiring real, specific consumption reductions (45-50% globally, 75-80% in rich countries) and active population stabilization, aimed at a long-term sustainable population Rees names directly as roughly one to two billion."@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Real, further citable material found but NOT built in this pass: Tomas Björkman (real TGS episode #48, directly cross-referenced), now built (2026-09-02); Julian Simon and the real, famous Simon-Ehrlich wager (already independently known via Paul Ehrlich's own real, still-deferred citations elsewhere in this graph); Albert Bartlett ('The New Flat Earth Society,' directly cited); Bjørn Lomborg (real, directly cited economist, subject of a real published Nature debate with Rees, not modeled as a Persona given his real, adversarial rather than collaborative relationship to this graph's own worldview)."@en ;
    .

