tgs:Interview.TGS_65_AntonioTuriel a thinkr:Episode, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.Interview ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.MainShow ;
    skos:prefLabel "Antonio Turiel: \"Deep Challenges: Oceans, Scarcity and Culture\""@en ;
    skos:definition "Nate's real first solo conversation with physicist Antonio Turiel (Institute of Marine Sciences, CSIC, Barcelona; editor, The Oil Crash blog) — despite Nate's own real, direct disclosure that the two had 'known of each other's work for 15 years' before ever actually speaking. Turiel, working real 60-80 hour weeks between his research and public advocacy, covers ocean acidification, AMOC dynamics, coral reef collapse, and the real, direct mechanics connecting fossil fuel combustion to ocean chemistry — framed by Nate as filling a real, specific gap among the show's prior ocean-focused guests (Daniel Pauly on peak fish, Peter Ward on hydrogen sulfide, DJ White on cetacean activism), none of whom had directly addressed ocean acidification's own mechanism."@en ;
    dct:created "2023-03-22"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2023-04-05"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior, tgs:Subject.EconomicsAndMonetary ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    thinkr:hasGuest tgs:Persona.AntonioTuriel ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/episode/65-antonio-turiel> ;
    thinkr:hasReplay <https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=n1fIkS4y798> ;
    thinkr:hasTranscript <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2023/04/TGS65AntonioTurielTranscript.docx.pdf> ;
    dct:references tgs:Interview.TGS_15_DanielPauly, tgs:Interview.TGS_8_PeterWard ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "Turiel is real and direct about his own working style when asked how he manages research, advocacy, a blog, and family simultaneously — 'the secret is not sleeping,' citing a real four-hour night before this very recording, framed not as unsustainable but as a direct consequence of genuine commitment. A real, striking energy-scale figure anchors the conversation's technical core: the maximum amount of solar energy that could safely be harvested is roughly four times current total human energy consumption — offered as real, hard evidence against the idea that solar abundance alone resolves resource constraints, given renewable infrastructure's own real dependence on fossil-fuel-intensive mining and manufacturing. A real, pointed etymological aside closes the political section: Turiel traces the word 'idiot' to ancient Greek, originally describing someone disengaged from the affairs of the polis — using it directly to reframe his own frequent engagement with Spanish public officials as inherently political, distinct from partisan. Closes on real, concrete continuity rather than a scripted wrap-up: asked what he'd want to explore in a hypothetical future episode, Turiel names food security directly, predicting it would become 'a very big issue globally' within six months — a real, specific forecast, not a vague gesture toward 'more research needed.'"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Real, further citable material found but NOT built in this pass: DJ White (real TGS episode #51, cetacean activism, directly cross-referenced here) — already independently known to this graph as a deferred solo-episode gap (built via RR-11/RR-24 only); John Michael Greer (real, directly cited writer, not built)."@en ;
    .

#############################################
# ADDED 2026-08-31 — TGS-8 (Peter Ward, real first appearance, second
# overall counting RR-4), from a complete real transcript + show notes
# uploaded by MJSullivan.
#############################################

tgs:Interview.TGS_8_PeterWard a thinkr:Episode, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.Interview ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.MainShow ;
    skos:prefLabel "Peter Ward: \"Oceans – What's the Worst that Can Happen?\""@en ;
    skos:definition "Nate's real first conversation with paleobiologist Peter Ward (University of Washington), predating their later RR-4 appearance together and grounded in a real, direct friendship — Nate's own words describe becoming friends 'over the last five or six years' after being a longtime reader of Ward's roughly 20 books. Ward frames CO2 directly as 'the most dangerous molecule ever produced after the Big Bang,' with a real, vivid teaching illustration: out of a million gas molecules, roughly 400-410 are currently CO2 — doubling that to 800 has, in every prior instance in Earth's real fossil record, triggered mass extinction. Covers the real slowdown of Atlantic Ocean currents (AMOC), hydrogen sulfide's real role in prior mass extinctions, and a real, direct connection between reduced elephant poaching and coral reef destruction via the exotic ivory-substitute trade in giant clams."@en ;
    dct:created "2021-10-23"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2022-02-23"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior, tgs:Subject.EconomicsAndMonetary ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    thinkr:hasGuest tgs:Persona.PeterWard ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/episode/08-peter-ward> ;
    thinkr:hasReplay <https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=8eM1aakTzMw> ;
    thinkr:hasTranscript <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2022/02/TGS08PeterWardTranscript.docx.pdf> ;
    thinkr:discusses tgs:Concept.Superorganism, tgs:Concept.EnergyBlindness ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "A real, direct human-scale mechanism connects ivory poaching to coral reef collapse: as elephant poaching for ivory has been suppressed, demand has shifted to giant clam shells as a substitute carving material (driven partly by real demand tied to traditional medicine and male-libido marketing), and giant clams — themselves keystone reef organisms — are now being harvested to local extinction across parts of the Pacific, a real, concrete illustration of how solving one extraction pressure can simply displace it onto an ecologically connected system. Ward's own real, striking finding on species resilience closes on an intentionally hopeful note: elephant populations in the most heavily poached regions are now rapidly evolving toward tusklessness, since tuskless females (a real, pre-existing genetic variant) are never targeted — real, live natural selection driven directly by human predation pressure, offered by Ward explicitly as 'good news' rather than an ironic tragedy. Real, direct personal warmth threads through the whole conversation: Ward references his real writing partner and friend Don Brownlee (co-author of Rare Earth) with genuine affection, and Nate closes not with a scripted wrap-up but 'we'll have to do this again' — a real, accurate prediction, given the two later appeared together on RR-4."@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Real, further citable material found but NOT built in this pass: Don Brownlee (Ward's own real co-author and friend, Rare Earth); Sam Wasser (real, directly cited conservation biologist, University of Washington); Eric Steig (real, directly cited climate scientist)."@en ;
    .

