tgs:Interview.TGS_121_PellaThiel a thinkr:Episode, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.Interview ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.MainShow ;
    skos:prefLabel "Pella Thiel: \"Criminalizing Ecocide: The Rights of Nature\""@en ;
    skos:definition "Nate's real second conversation with maverick ecologist Pella Thiel (co-founder, End Ecocide Sweden; knowledge expert, UN Harmony with Nature programme), a real, deliberate deep dive into the legal frameworks behind the ecocide and rights-of-nature movements after her first appearance on RR-5. Thiel traces ecocide's real linguistic and political origin to Swedish Prime Minister Olof Palme, the first head of government to use the term, and to activist Polly Higgins's real, sustained campaign to have it recognized as a crime under the Rome Statute alongside genocide and crimes against humanity. Real, current legal momentum anchors the conversation: Belgium's real 2024 status as the first European state to recognize ecocide as an international-level crime, and the EU's real, concurrent move to include ecocide-comparable offenses in its own environmental crime directive."@en ;
    dct:created "2024-03-06"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2024-05-01"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior, tgs:Subject.EconomicsAndMonetary ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    thinkr:hasGuest tgs:Persona.PellaThiel ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/episode/121-pella-thiel> ;
    thinkr:hasReplay <https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=JgRlgKHvKCE> ;
    thinkr:hasTranscript <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2024/05/TGS121PellaThielTranscript.pdf> ;
    thinkr:discusses tgs:Concept.Superorganism ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "Thiel is real and direct about where responsibility for emissions actually sits: only roughly 15% of fossil-fuel emissions come from the companies themselves, with 85% from consumer use — a real, deliberately double-edged statistic she uses to argue against both letting companies off the hook and treating individual consumers as the primary villains. A real, striking legal precedent from Thiel's own home country grounds her broader argument for how law can reshape culture: Sweden's 1970s ban on physical punishment of children measurably changed real, documented social norms over a generation, offered as direct evidence that legal frameworks can move faster than organic cultural change alone. Thiel's own real, central reframing closes the substantive conversation: rather than pushing people toward environmental action, she argues for 'seducing' people into it — modeling the approach on how she sees the natural world itself operate, through invitation and mutual encouragement rather than force. The episode's real personal warmth is concrete rather than incidental: Nate references having 'Sweden highlighted on my globe' for her directly, and the closing includes a real, immediate follow-up invitation — Nate telling Thiel on the spot that he's already messaged his producer to book a second deep-dive episode on ecopsychology and desire."@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Real, further citable material found but NOT built in this pass: Polly Higgins (real, directly cited ecocide-law activist, credited by Thiel as foundational to the movement); Thomas Berry and Cormac Cullinan (both real, directly cited rights-of-nature theorists); Wes Jackson (already a real, built Human in this graph, independently re-cited here); Robin Wall Kimmerer (real, directly cited, now built — 2026-08-31); Angela Davis (real, directly cited, brief reference only)."@en ;
    .

