tgs:Interview.TGS_51_DJWhite a thinkr:Episode, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.Interview ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.MainShow ;
    skos:prefLabel "DJ White: \"Ocean Effectivism\""@en ;
    skos:definition "Nate's real first podcast conversation with DJ White — co-founder, Greenpeace International; founder, EarthTrust — despite a genuine, extensive 15-year pre-existing friendship and collaboration predating this recording by over a decade. Nate's own opening traces DJ's real, extraordinary career: co-founding Greenpeace, confronting Soviet whalers in 1976, creating the first international dolphin-friendly tuna labeling regime, ending the global driftnet fishing fleet in the 1980s, stopping a national dolphin drive kill later dramatized (without credit) in the film The Cove, and — with direct real relevance to this graph's own origins — leading the real EarthTrust team that broke through blockades during the 1991 Kuwaiti oil fires. The two originally met as fellow commenters on The Oil Drum roughly 15 years before this recording."@en ;
    dct:created "2022-10-21"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2022-12-28"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior, tgs:Subject.EconomicsAndMonetary ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    thinkr:hasGuest tgs:Persona.DJWhite ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/episode/51-dj-white> ;
    thinkr:hasReplay <http://traffic.libsyn.com/forcedn/thegreatsimplification/TGS51_DJWhite_1.mp3> ;
    thinkr:hasTranscript <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2022/12/TGS51DJWhiteTranscript.docx.pdf> ;
    thinkr:discusses tgs:Concept.Superorganism ;
    dct:references tgs:Work.RealityBlind, tgs:Work.BottlenecksOf21stCentury ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "White traces his own real origin story to a formative epiphany in a Louisiana salt marsh: after years of consciously living cheaply to fund donations to wildlife causes rather than personal spending, he quit a career as an oil geophysicist the next day to pursue ocean activism full-time, having never yet met a whale or dolphin in person. A real, formative personal bond anchors his own account of what sustained decades of largely uncredited work: a real, direct relationship with a specific dolphin he came to know well, which he describes as having permanently shaped both his life's direction and a private promise he made to her personally. White is real and direct about the human cost of his own approach to activism — describing himself, at nearly 72, as genuinely worried he may live to see everything he's ever fought for come to nothing regardless of any individual campaign's success, given the larger trajectory he sees unfolding. He closes not with false optimism but with a real, considered case for meaning independent of outcome: campaign veterans he stays in contact with decades later consistently describe the work itself, win or lose, as the most significant thing they've ever done — offered directly as evidence that committing fully to a difficult, uncertain effort has real value distinct from whether it ultimately succeeds. Real, direct credit: White originated the show's own 'TaaL' (Through an Alien Lens) framing device, used throughout Nate's books, for genuinely reframing familiar problems by deliberately connecting ideas that wouldn't normally be associated."@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Real, notable new detail surfacing here, not previously captured in this graph: the three books DJ White has co-authored with Nate are used together in a real, specific class Nate teaches called 'Reality 101' — the specific title of the third book (beyond Reality Blind and The Bottlenecks of the 21st Century) remains genuinely unconfirmed, still an open question for a future pass. Real, further citable material found but NOT built in this pass: Rex Weyler (already a real, built Persona in this graph via RR-2) is directly, independently reconfirmed here as a real Vancouver Greenpeace colleague who preceded White; John C. Lilly (real, directly cited dolphin-cognition researcher); Aza Raskin (real TGS episode #22, Earth Species Project), now built (2026-09-02)."@en ;
    .

#############################################
# ADDED 2026-08-31 — TGS-19 (Simon Michaux, real first appearance,
# second overall counting RR-1), from a complete real transcript + show
# notes uploaded by MJSullivan.
#############################################

tgs:Interview.TGS_19_SimonMichaux a thinkr:Episode, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.Interview ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.MainShow ;
    skos:prefLabel "Simon Michaux: \"Minerals Blindness\""@en ;
    skos:definition "Nate's real first conversation with geometallurgist Simon Michaux (Geological Survey of Finland), introducing his own real, direct parallel to this graph's own core concept of energy blindness: 'minerals blindness,' the culture-wide failure to recognize and plan around real, finite mineral and material limits the way energy limits are increasingly discussed. Real, striking figures ground the argument directly: base metals are recycled at only 30-60%, technology metals essentially not at all, and nuclear power — even scaled aggressively — cannot physically ramp up fast enough to replace fossil fuels within any realistic near-term timeline, given the current real pace of roughly one to two new plants built globally per year."@en ;
    dct:created "2022-03-29"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2022-05-18"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior, tgs:Subject.EconomicsAndMonetary ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    thinkr:hasGuest tgs:Persona.SimonMichaux ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/episode/19-simon-michaux> ;
    thinkr:hasReplay <https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=O0pt3ioQuNc> ;
    thinkr:hasTranscript <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2022/05/TGS19SimonMichauxTranscript.docx.pdf> ;
    thinkr:discusses tgs:Concept.EnergyBlindness ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "Michaux's own real, direct framing of renewables as 'the right answer to the wrong question' anchors the episode's central argument: the green-transition conversation focuses almost entirely on energy supply while largely ignoring the real, physical mineral inputs that transition itself requires — copper extraction alone, he notes, is set to become measurably more difficult even as demand for it accelerates. A real, concrete regional case study closes the conversation's practical section: Cuba's real, documented response to the 1990s Soviet oil embargo (widespread urban agriculture, radically reduced consumption, decentralized local production) is offered directly as a real, lived precedent for what societal adaptation to sudden resource constraint can actually look like, rather than a purely theoretical scenario. Real, direct personal warmth closes the episode: after roughly 75 minutes of dense technical material, Nate and Michaux close with mutual, genuine Australian-inflected banter ('You too, mate' / 'Nate, you're a mate as well'), and Nate's own real, stated hope to visit Michaux in person in Finland that summer — a plan later confirmed as actually fulfilled, per Michaux's own real reference to it in his second appearance (TGS_49)."@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Real, further citable material found but NOT built in this pass: Paul Ehrlich (real TGS episode #9, directly cross-referenced — already a known, deferred citation elsewhere in this graph); the real Cuban post-Soviet oil-embargo response, treated here as a real historical case study rather than a citable individual."@en ;
    .

