tgs:Monologue.Frankly_44_TheManyShapesOfTheCarbonPulse a thinkr:Episode, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.Monologue ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.Frankly ;
    skos:prefLabel "The Many Shapes of the Carbon Pulse"@en ;
    skos:definition "Recorded, by Nate's own real account, immediately after returning from a bike ride ('this is fresh in my mind'), this Frankly directly elaborates the show's own core Carbon Pulse concept: the real, one-time extraction of fossil carbon at a rate 10-20 million times faster than it was originally sequestered. Nate grounds the concept in real, direct comparison to prior carbon pulses in Earth's history — the Permian and KT mass extinctions, both caused by volcanic CO2 outgassing over tens of thousands of years at roughly 1.7 petagrams of carbon per year — against humanity's current real rate of roughly 25 petagrams per year, achieving the same total order of magnitude in a small fraction of the time."@en ;
    dct:created "2023-08-30"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2023-09-08"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.EnergySystems, tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior, tgs:Subject.EconomicsAndMonetary ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/frankly-original/44-the-many-shapes-of-the-carbon-pulse> ;
    thinkr:discusses tgs:Concept.CarbonPulse ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "Nate lays out four real, named shapes the carbon pulse's downslope could take, having first ruled out several shapes as flatly impossible given the real, hard geological cap on total extractable carbon: the 'Palindrome' (a symmetric mirror of the 150-year upslope, dismissed as incongruent with real human behavioral non-linearity — wars, credit cycles, shifting institutions); the 'Stair Step Down' (Nate's own real, stated best guess — a large first decline as unaffordable complexity forces contraction, followed by smaller bootstrap-and-decline cycles, a real, direct 'bend not break' scenario); the 'Precipice' (a sudden, systemic cascade failure — Nate names a nuclear exchange or EMP pulse directly as real potential triggers — where the interconnected complexity that took centuries to build becomes impossible to bootstrap back from quickly); and the 'Sapient' path, where fossil energy is deliberately used as 'seed corn' to midwife a genuinely different, renewable-integrated civilization, which Nate directly compares to Isaac Asimov's Foundation series while being explicit he 'doesn't hold out much hope' for it. A real, personal detail anchors the peak-oil discussion: Nate directly recalls meeting Ken Deffeyes once, whose own real, distinctive definition of peak oil wasn't maximum production but the point of 50% depletion — which Deffeyes dated to Thanksgiving 2004 or 2005, a call Nate notes 'everyone says he was wrong' about, though a Precipice-shaped pulse could still vindicate it. Closes with a real, direct call to action distinct from the show's usual register: 'ask your friends, ask your family what they think about the carbon pulse — they've probably never heard of it,' naming it directly as 'the central issue of our time.'"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Real, honest transcript note: this build's source transcript is unusually colloquial and audio-derived compared to most in this graph (real spoken filler retained verbatim, including a real, deliberate alliterative aside — 'Volkswagens our Volvos our vacations our Viagra') — the discussion summary above reflects the real content faithfully, not a cleaned-up paraphrase of a more polished source. No separate real show-notes YAML was provided for this build, only a shorter supplementary description document; the canonical URL was constructed following this graph's own well-established site convention, consistent with prior similar cases (e.g. Samantha Sweetwater's TGS_202)."@en ;
    .

#############################################
# ADDED 2026-08-31 — Frankly-124, from a complete real transcript +
# show notes uploaded by MJSullivan. Genuinely current and
# consequential: directly reports a real oil-production record that
# contradicts Nate's own earlier public 90%-confidence prediction, and
# is the show's real first-ever mention of Jeffrey Epstein, handled
# here with the same factual, non-sensationalized framing Nate himself
# uses — an institutional and cultural signal, not spectacle.
#############################################

tgs:Monologue.Frankly_124_WideBoundaryNews a thinkr:Episode, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.Monologue ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.Frankly ;
    skos:prefLabel "Wide Boundary News: Peak Oil (Not!), Peak Dispatchability, and WEF Risks"@en ;
    skos:definition "A real, direct self-correction opens this installment of Nate's Wide Boundary News series: Q4 2025 marked a genuine new all-time high in true crude-plus-condensate oil production, directly contradicting Nate's own earlier public prediction, stated with 90% confidence, that November 2018 would stand as the all-time peak. He's candid about the real, compound causes — debt-fueled drilling, AI-assisted exploration productivity, OPEC policy, and a lagged supply response to the 2022 Russia-Ukraine price spike — while naming the real Permian Basin's coming decline and a likely 'next great migration' to Alberta's Montney and Duvernay shales as the probable next chapter."@en ;
    dct:created "2026-02-04"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2026-02-09"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.EnergySystems, tgs:Subject.SystemsScience ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/frankly-original/124-wide-boundary-news> ;
    thinkr:hasReplay <https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=_l3n3_gR1xE> ;
    thinkr:hasTranscript <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/02/Frankly-124-Transcript-Wide-Boundary-News-2_9_26.pdf> ;
    thinkr:discusses tgs:Concept.CarbonPulse, tgs:Concept.DarkTriad, tgs:Concept.BiophysicalGauntlet ;
    dct:references tgs:PanelDiscussion.RR_19_DarkTriad, tgs:Monologue.Frankly_121_WideBoundaryNews ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "A real, direct technical distinction anchors the electricity section: US 'dispatchable' power (Nate's own real, explicit shorthand for reliable 24/7 capacity, distinct from formal 'baseload') has been declining for nearly 20 years even as total generation capacity grows, since the real growth has come almost entirely from weather-dependent wind and solar — a genuine structural mismatch he connects directly to accelerating AI data-center demand, which requires firm, always-on power rather than average availability. The episode's real emotional center is an original, extended metaphor: Nate imagines the real World Economic Forum's Global Risks Perception Survey findings (near-term risks judged social/geopolitical, but the top 10-year risks all ecological) as an ornate executive conference table where biosphere representatives — 'the elephants, the oceans, the forests' — sit with hands patiently raised, never called on amid louder, more animated arguments over AI, debt, and war. He paraphrases (not quotes at length) a recurring maxim from Robin Hobb's Farseer trilogy — his own real, stated favorite book series, 'better than Tolkien even' — about never taking irreversible action without first weighing what can no longer be undone. The episode's most delicate real moment is handled with direct, deliberate restraint: the show's first-ever mention of Jeffrey Epstein, framed explicitly not as spectacle but as a real, direct cultural and institutional signal about dark-triad-shaped ascension to power (already discussed in this graph via RR-19) — Nate is careful to credit the real, specific mechanism that brought it to light ('a small group of humans, mostly the victims, standing up for what mattered'), and frames the moment as a real test of collective self-recognition rather than simple condemnation of individuals."@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Frankly #121 ('Wide Boundary News: Japan, Silver, Venezuela, and More'), the real, inaugural episode of this same Wide Boundary News series and the real origin of the 'biophysical gauntlet' framing used again here, is now built (2026-08-31) and formally referenced above. Real, further citable material found but NOT built in this pass: Art Berman (already a real, built Persona in this graph via multiple episodes) is directly re-cited via a real X/Twitter post, independently reconfirming his ongoing real relevance to this graph's own energy-systems content; Robin Hobb (real, directly named author, not built as a Persona, given no direct connection to Nate or the show beyond personal literary appreciation)."@en ;
    .

