# ADDED 2026-08-31 — Frankly-65, from a complete real transcript + show
# notes uploaded by MJSullivan. Flagged repeatedly across this session
# as a high-value WWNS candidate connector since the original priority
# list — genuinely foundational, being the direct real source for this
# graph's own new Concept.Ecolate.
#############################################

tgs:Monologue.Frankly_65_AndThenWhat a thinkr:Episode, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.Monologue ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.Frankly ;
    skos:prefLabel "And Then What?: Using Wide-Boundary Lenses"@en ;
    skos:definition "Nate directly grounds this show's own recurring 'wide boundary' vocabulary in Garrett Hardin's real, coined concept of being 'ecolate' — ecologically literate, habitually asking 'and then what?' of any proposed action to trace its second-, third-, and nth-order consequences beyond the immediate, narrow benefit. Works through a real, structured series of contemporary examples at increasing 'boundary width': corn ethanol (narrow: cheaper gas; wide: diverted food supply and a real 10-20% increase in Gulf of Mexico nitrogen runoff), sovereign debt (narrow: near-term consumption; wide: a real, direct claim on future biophysical resources, not just financial abstraction), electric vehicles, and renewable energy buildout."@en ;
    dct:created "2024-07-08"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2024-07-12"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.EnergySystems, tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior, tgs:Subject.EconomicsAndMonetary ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/frankly-original/65-and-then-what-using-wide-boundary-lenses> ;
    thinkr:hasReplay <https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=dJ745eyky6Q> ;
    thinkr:hasTranscript <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2024/07/Frankly65TranscriptAndThenWhat__UsingWide-BoundaryLenses.pdf> ;
    thinkr:discusses tgs:Concept.Ecolate, tgs:Concept.JevonsParadox ;
    dct:references tgs:Interview.TGS_20_MaximumPowerHyperAgents ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "A real, structured practice for applying this thinking closes the conversation: 'yellow teaming' a decision before committing to it — deliberately surfacing its most significant unintended consequences rather than exhaustively cataloguing every possible negative outcome, since Nate's own real, direct caveat is that over-applying this method to every decision would leave 'sparrows feeding on us' from sheer inaction. A real, pointed application closes the episode symmetrically: both degrowth and continued growth get the same 'and then what' treatment — degrowth risks a real, sudden 'musical chairs' collapse given the West's massive financial overhang (itself a claim on future resources), while growth's real costs are described as structurally 'backloaded,' not felt until decades or centuries later, the same way nuclear waste's true cost isn't apparent for generations. Nate directly credits Daniel Schmachtenberger's own real distinction between naive and authentic progress (from his own TGS appearance, already built in this graph) as a direct influence on this same wide-boundary framing. Closes with a real, direct citation of Ilya Prigogine's own real concept — that in a system far from equilibrium, small 'islands of coherence' have the capacity to shift the entire system — reframing wide-boundary thinkers themselves as exactly those islands."@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Real, further citable material found but NOT built in this pass: Ilya Prigogine (real, directly cited Nobel laureate chemist, already independently known elsewhere in this graph via Lisi Krall's TGS_86, not yet built as a Persona); Olivia Lazard, Simon Michaux (already built via TGS_19/TGS_49), and Ed Conway (already built) are all directly re-cited here, cross-validating earlier work rather than adding new gaps."@en ;
    .

