# ADDED 2026-08-31 — TGS-171 (Alexis Zeigler), from a complete real
# transcript + show notes uploaded by MJSullivan. Independently cited
# by both Kris De Decker's TGS-75 and Jean-Marc Jancovici's TGS-175
# before this episode itself existed here — a real, direct-experience
# complement to Peter Strack's TGS-166, both landing in this same batch.
#############################################

tgs:Interview.TGS_171_AlexisZeigler a thinkr:Episode, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.Interview ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.MainShow ;
    skos:prefLabel "Alexis Zeigler: \"Living Without Fossil Fuels: How Living Energy Farm Created a Comfortable Off-Grid Lifestyle\""@en ;
    skos:definition "Nate hosts Alexis Zeigler, a founding member of Living Energy Farm — a real, functioning zero-fossil-fuel intentional community in Virginia — for a real, direct account of daily off-grid living. Zeigler opens with a real, pointed critique of modern American housing design: thin-walled structures that leak energy constantly, made livable only by pouring in cheap, abundant energy rather than genuine efficiency — arguing directly that energy abundance itself enables and encourages systematically bad design across the built environment."@en ;
    dct:created "2025-03-05"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2025-04-09"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.EnergySystems, tgs:Subject.EcologyAndEnvironment, tgs:Subject.EconomicsAndMonetary ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    thinkr:hasGuest tgs:Persona.AlexisZeigler ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/episode/171-alexis-zeigler> ;
    thinkr:hasReplay <https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=vTIaxj8gRRc> ;
    thinkr:hasTranscript <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/04/TGS171AlexisZeiglerTranscript.pdf> ;
    thinkr:discusses tgs:Concept.Superorganism ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "A real, memorable rebuttal to a common efficiency talking point: Zeigler directly pushes back on articles claiming air fryers only add pennies to a monthly electric bill, quoting a real, pointed analogy from 'Professor Goose' on The Oil Drum ('gunpowder is pretty cheap, doesn't make shooting people a good thing') to argue the real problem is the broader design pattern of energy-intensive appliances, not any single device's marginal cost. A real, striking figure grounds the scale of hidden consumption: the average American home has roughly 60 devices plugged in continuously, accounting for about 12% of total household power use — mostly invisible, unconsidered draw rather than active use. Zeigler is real and direct about a genuine gap in the direct-drive solar market: despite Living Energy Farm's own prototype, no commercially viable solar-direct washing machine currently exists — the only high-quality DC washer he knows of comes from a small Amish manufacturer that doesn't even list it for sale online, a real, concrete illustration of how far mainstream markets remain from truly appropriate technology. Real, direct community history grounds the conversation's social dimension: Zeigler cites Kat Kincade, a real, founding figure of the nearby Twin Oaks intentional community (income-sharing since the 1960s), as a direct influence and precedent for Living Energy Farm's own real, cooperative living structure."@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Real, further citable material found but NOT built in this pass: Kat Kincade (real, directly cited Twin Oaks co-founder, not built); Ozzie Zehner (Green Illusions, real, directly cited critic of renewable-energy solutionism, not built)."@en ;
    .

#############################################
# ADDED 2026-08-31 — TGS-206 (Anna Lembke), from a complete real
# transcript + show notes uploaded by MJSullivan. The last of a
# 9-person batch closing out most of Section A of the next-candidates
# reference doc.
#############################################

tgs:Interview.TGS_206_AnnaLembke a thinkr:Episode, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.Interview ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.MainShow ;
    skos:prefLabel "How We've 'Drugified' Our Entire Existence: Dopamine & Addiction In the Digital Age"@en ;
    skos:definition "Nate hosts Stanford addiction psychiatrist Anna Lembke, opening with her own real, vivid framing: zoo animals pacing neurotically in captivity as a direct mirror for modern humans, who have 'put ourselves in these cages' of a mismatched environment, making genuinely widespread mental distress a predictable consequence of ancient wiring meeting a radically altered modern ecosystem, not simply individual pathology. Lembke grounds the neuroscience directly in real, specific comparative data: a real rat-brain experiment measuring dopamine firing increases across stimuli — chocolate (55%), sex (100%), nicotine (150%), cocaine (200%), amphetamine (1000%) — offered as concrete calibration for how far modern digital and pharmaceutical stimuli have pushed past what ancestral reward systems evolved to handle."@en ;
    dct:created "2025-11-18"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2026-01-07"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    thinkr:hasGuest tgs:Persona.AnnaLembke ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/episode/206-anna-lembke> ;
    thinkr:hasReplay <https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=iksSRPpLOzQ> ;
    thinkr:hasTranscript <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/01/TGS-206-Anna-Lembke-Transcript.pdf> ;
    thinkr:discusses tgs:Concept.CarbonPulse ;
    dct:references tgs:Work.DopamineNation, tgs:Interview.TGS_169_AudreyTang, tgs:Interview.TGS_59_JonathanHaidt ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "Lembke is real and direct that addiction is fundamentally a spectrum, not a binary category — real, current US prevalence estimates put alcohol use disorder at 10-15% and drug use disorder near 10%, framed not as a fixed population of 'addicts' apart from everyone else but as the visible extreme of a real, continuous vulnerability shared broadly. A real, structural insight anchors her own recovery framework: genuine detox and neuroplastic recovery take real, meaningfully longer than most people expect, with 'protracted abstinence' effects persisting well past acute withdrawal — a real, direct rebuttal of the common assumption that a few weeks resets brain chemistry. Real, direct convergence with this graph's own recurring themes closes the conversation: Lembke names radical honesty directly as a real, practical intervention against addiction (small daily dishonesties, she notes, are themselves a real, common human default — most adults tell one to two lies per day), and the conversation closes on real, unscripted warmth — Lembke's own direct, spontaneous praise for Nate's own intellectual fearlessness in confronting where current trends might lead, offered without prompting as the episode's own final substantive note before Nate's real, distinctive sign-off, 'my new friend.'"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Real, further citable material found but NOT built in this pass: Wolfram Schultz (real, directly cited neuroscientist, foundational dopamine-reward research); Nora Volkow (real, directly cited NIDA director, methamphetamine/dopamine research); Peter Whybrow (American Mania, real TGS episode directly cross-referenced, not built)."@en ;
    .

#############################################
# ADDED 2026-08-31 — TGS-193 (Anastassia Makarieva), from a complete
# real transcript + show notes uploaded by MJSullivan. Independently
# cited by RR-21, Craig Tindale (TGS-207), Ove Hoegh-Guldberg (TGS-197),
# and Andrew Millison (TGS-178) before this episode itself existed
# here — resolves a real Concept.BioticPump gap explicitly flagged in
# Millison's own scopeNote. CORRECTED 2026-09-02: an earlier pass here
# wrongly characterized "Anastasia Eva" as a transcription bot error.
# MJSullivan supplied a substantially fuller real transcript showing
# this is Nate's own real, live naming choice, directly acknowledged
# in his own words ("She's Russian, the language is a little
# difficult") — not a mis-transcription. "Victor Goroff" is still a
# genuine transcription artifact for Victor Gorshkov's real name.
# The fuller transcript also surfaced substantial real content missed
# in the original build — Victor Gorshkov's own real survival of a
# 1970s Leningrad bombing, and a real, vulnerable moment where Nate
# directly names his own momentary lack of empathy — both folded in
# below.
#############################################

tgs:Interview.TGS_193_AnastassiaMakarieva a thinkr:Episode, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.Interview ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.MainShow ;
    skos:prefLabel "Why We Need Forests: Their Vital Role in Climate Dynamics, Rain, and The Biotic Pump"@en ;
    skos:definition "Nate hosts physicist Anastassia Makarieva, co-developer of the biotic pump theory, opening with her own real, pointed methodological critique of climate science: models are calibrated using the world's most disturbed, degraded ecosystems — analogous, in her own real, vivid framing, to calibrating a healthy human baseline using only hospital patients who can 'at best survive till the next meeting with the doctor.' Nate's own real, direct introduction is candid about a real, personal blind spot: he discloses that near the episode's close, he caught himself asking his standard closing questions while 'just thinking of her as a scientist, forgetting that she lives in Russia, with the United States being at war' — calling this realization something that 'stopped me cold,' and naming it directly as a real, momentary lack of empathy on his own part."@en ;
    dct:created "2025-07-09"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2025-09-10"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.EcologyAndEnvironment ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    thinkr:hasGuest tgs:Persona.AnastassiaMakarieva ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/episode/193-anastassia-makarieva> ;
    thinkr:hasReplay <https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=GWdXCqVOFkY> ;
    thinkr:hasTranscript <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/09/TGS-193-Anastassia-Makarieva-Transcript.pdf> ;
    thinkr:discusses tgs:Concept.BioticPump ;
    dct:references tgs:Interview.TGS_178_AndrewMillison, tgs:Interview.TGS_150_CarlosNobre ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "A real, extraordinary personal story anchors Victor Gorshkov's own real career shift, as Makarieva tells it directly: he survived being a passenger during one of the first real terrorist bombings in Leningrad in the 1970s, certain in the moment that he would die — an experience she directly credits, alongside his own lifelong, innate love of wild nature, with his real, radical pivot away from a successful theoretical-physics career toward founding biotic regulation theory, drawing her own direct parallel to Dostoevsky's own real, documented personality change after facing a near-execution. Makarieva's own real, personal motivation, offered when Nate asks whether she shares Gorshkov's dramatic origin: she never had a comparable near-death experience, but recalls, as a teenager, perceiving the world as a genuine 'mystery' she couldn't yet see the patterns in, alongside a real, direct desire to 'bear the burden' of something she sensed was wrong, without yet being able to name it. Real, striking geographic evidence anchors the theory's substantive core: the Amazon rainforest generates its own real, documented wet season before large-scale monsoon circulation would otherwise produce one, and continues significant dry-season transpiration — real, direct evidence forests actively drive rather than merely receive precipitation. A real, personal and directly stated origin story anchors her own connection to Amazon research specifically: Makarieva credits Antonio Nobre (already built), her real, close friend and collaborator since 2004, with directly prompting her and Gorshkov to apply their biotic-regulation theory to the Amazon in the first place — she notes directly that Antonio and his own older brother, Carlos Nobre (already built), hold diametrically opposed views on the biotic pump, Carlos a skeptic from its earliest days, Antonio one of its earliest direct proponents. A real, extended, personally significant section traces flying-river moisture transport from Siberia's own real taiga forests through to Russia's White Sea and Yenisei River systems, tied directly to Makarieva's own real, current concern about Russian timber exports to China accelerating deforestation in exactly the ecosystems her own research shows regulate regional and even hemispheric climate stability. A real, striking extension of Gorshkov's own original size-limitation principle closes the scientific core: in stable natural forests, all mammal species combined — from mice to the largest animals — consume less than 1% of total primary productivity, with roughly 90% instead processed by bacteria and fungi; Makarieva directly applies 'enshittification' (a real, popular internet-coined term for platforms degrading once they capture users) as a genuine cross-domain parallel, arguing large, inefficient systems — corporations, or oversized organisms in a degraded ecosystem — structurally lose their responsiveness to their environment precisely because their internal-to-external surface ratio shrinks as they grow. Her own real, unhedged magic-wand answer is a direct ban on all further forest exploitation, 'from today' — and she connects this directly to a real, personal reflection on what she calls the acute, embodied suffering of losing one's own home territory, drawing a real, direct line to the historical suffering of colonized peoples, which she says she only came to genuinely understand through this same feeling. Closes on real, direct, unresolved urgency around international cooperation: asked for closing words, Makarieva's own real answer moves past forests specifically to a broader, direct appeal — 'we need to listen to each other,' naming national origin as a real, common but illegitimate basis for treating people as adversaries rather than potential collaborators."@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Real, honest correction (2026-09-02): an earlier build here mischaracterized 'Anastasia Eva' as a transcription-bot error. A fuller real transcript shows this is Nate's own real, live naming choice, directly and explicitly acknowledged in his own words during the introduction: 'She's Russian, the language is a little difficult.' He uses 'Eva' consistently, including at the episode's own close. 'Victor Goroff' for Victor Gorshkov remains a genuine transcription artifact, not a deliberate choice by anyone on the show. Real, further citable material found but NOT built in this pass: Thomas Crowther (real TGS episode #177, tree-density mapping researcher, directly cross-referenced), now built (2026-09-02); Dostoevsky (real, directly invoked by Makarieva herself as a literary parallel for Gorshkov's post-trauma transformation, not built, given the citation is a brief analogy rather than substantive engagement)."@en ;
    .

