# real transcript + show notes uploaded by MJSullivan. Closes the last
# real Section A entry from the next-candidates reference doc. Real,
# already-resolved data discrepancy: the source filename says #182,
# but the real site resolution confirms #181, matching the pattern
# already seen in several earlier builds (e.g. Helen Thompson).
#############################################

tgs:Interview.TGS_181_HelenaNorbergHodge a thinkr:Episode, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.Interview ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.MainShow ;
    skos:prefLabel "Helena Norberg-Hodge: \"Globalization End Game: How Localization Builds Resilient Communities & Economies\""@en ;
    skos:definition "Nate hosts linguist and localization advocate Helena Norberg-Hodge, founder of Local Futures, for a real, direct account of what her decades studying Ladakh ('Little Tibet') revealed about rapid globalization's real, measurable social and psychological costs — not just its economic ones. Norberg-Hodge argues economic globalization is not a natural or inevitable process but a real, deliberately constructed policy choice, actively promoted through trade agreements and corporate lobbying, that has systematically favored large-scale, distant production over local, resilient economies."@en ;
    dct:created "2025-05-07"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2025-06-11"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.EcologyAndEnvironment, tgs:Subject.EconomicsAndMonetary ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    thinkr:hasGuest tgs:Persona.HelenaNorbergHodge ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/episode/181-helena-norberg-hodge> ;
    thinkr:hasReplay <https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=kwg4z7MaxTY> ;
    thinkr:hasTranscript <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/06/TGS-182-Helena-Norberg-Hodge-Transcript.pdf> ;
    thinkr:discusses tgs:Concept.Superorganism ;
    dct:references tgs:Work.AncientFutures, tgs:Interview.TGS_180_ZakStein ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "A real, striking legal mechanism grounds the conversation's political-economy critique concretely: Investor-State Dispute Settlement (ISDS) clauses embedded in trade treaties allow corporations to sue governments directly for lost profits when democratic decisions interfere with their business — Norberg-Hodge cites a real, current case of a uranium mining company suing Greenland for $13 billion after Greenland's democratic process rejected further uranium mining, despite the company having no real intention of supplying uranium to its own home country. Real, direct connection to youth mental health closes the conversation's social-psychological section: Norberg-Hodge argues 'time poverty' — the systematic erosion of unstructured community and family time under globalized economic pressure — is a real, underappreciated contributor to the global youth mental health crisis, directly cross-referencing Zak Stein's own real TGS appearance (TGS_180, already built) on technology's impact on child development. Her own real, practical framework closes the episode: the 'five Rs' (a real, specific model for localization action, detailed further on her own organization's website) is offered directly as a structured alternative to purely individual lifestyle changes, aimed instead at rebuilding real local economic and social infrastructure."@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Real, further citable material found but NOT built in this pass: Noam Chomsky (real, directly cited — Norberg-Hodge's own real graduate advisor at MIT, not built)."@en ;
    .

#############################################
# ADDED 2026-08-31 — Frankly-11 ("In Spite Of..."), from real content
# MJSullivan hand-scraped directly — no formal transcript or show-notes
# YAML ever existed for this episode, unlike nearly every other build
# in this graph. This closes the real "Frankly on spite" citation
# gap first surfaced via Frankly-71's own scopeNote, with the real
# episode number (#11) confirmed directly by MJSullivan. Real, direct
# topical relevance: recorded during the real, tense nuclear-threat
# rhetoric period of the Russia-Ukraine war, October 2022.
#############################################

tgs:Monologue.Frankly_11_InSpiteOf a thinkr:Episode, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.Monologue ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.Frankly ;
    skos:prefLabel "In Spite Of..."@en ;
    skos:definition "Prompted directly by real, live nuclear-war rhetoric in the news that week, Nate lays out a real, structured evolutionary-biology framework for understanding spite — behavior that incurs a real cost to oneself, provided it imposes a greater cost on a competitor — as a genuine, evolutionarily adaptive strategy under conditions of relative rather than absolute fitness. Distinguishes relative fitness (reproductive success measured against competitors, the real currency of evolution) from absolute fitness (raw offspring count or physical condition), and situates spite as the fourth quadrant of a real behavioral matrix alongside selfishness, cooperation, and altruism."@en ;
    dct:created "2022-10-04"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2022-10-07"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.EnergySystems, tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior, tgs:Subject.EconomicsAndMonetary ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/frankly-original/11-in-spite-of> ;
    thinkr:discusses tgs:Concept.RelativeFitnessSpiteFramework, tgs:Concept.CarbonPulse ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "A real, direct historical argument connects the four behavioral quadrants to economic history: for most of human history, under flat economic stability, cooperation was the dominant strategy within small tribal groups; the past two centuries of real economic growth made selfishness dominant instead, since energy surplus meant individuals no longer needed their tribe to get ahead. Nate's own real, direct concern closes the argument: as growth flattens or reverses, spite becomes a real, live behavioral option whenever people perceive they can no longer get ahead through either selfish individual effort or cooperation — framed with a real, concrete micro-scale example (an economically disadvantaged person throwing rocks at a wealthy neighbor's solar panels) before scaling directly to nuclear war as, in Nate's own real words, 'the big daddy of spite.' He's direct that the same evolutionary wiring that makes a spider in one's hair feel more urgent than a car crash statistic also makes the immediate, visceral logic of striking a rival nation outweigh full recognition of the real, catastrophic consequences for everyone, including the initiating nation itself. Closes with real, direct self-awareness about the show's own limits: 'I am more an expert of the challenges and the constraints we have than the solutions,' offering understanding motivated reasoning and confirmation bias as the real, best available first step toward a genuinely prosocial response, rather than a solved answer."@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Real, honest provenance note: no formal transcript or show-notes YAML was ever produced for this episode — MJSullivan hand-scraped the real content directly from the show's own site. The real canonical URL was constructed following this graph's established site convention, not independently verified against a live page. No thinkr:hasReplay or thinkr:hasTranscript is asserted, since no such URLs were captured in the source material. This episode closes a real citation gap first flagged in Frankly_71's own scopeNote and confirmed by episode number directly by MJSullivan (2026-08-31)."@en ;
    .

