# ADDED 2026-08-31 — Frankly-114 ("Directional Advice for the (More
# Than) Human Predicament"), from a complete real transcript + show
# notes uploaded by MJSullivan. Genuinely comprehensive — Nate's own
# real, explicit synthesis of "what do we do," structured as 6
# categories x 3 subcategories (18 directional items total). CORRECTED
# 2026-09-02: this transcript renders Anastassia Makarieva's name as
# "Anastasia Ava" — originally flagged here as a bot transcription
# error, but per TGS_193's own corrected scopeNote, this is Nate's
# own real, live, admittedly-difficult pronunciation of her name
# ("Eva" in TGS_193, "Ava" here), not a transcription artifact.
#############################################

tgs:Monologue.Frankly_114_DirectionalAdvice a thinkr:Episode, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.Monologue ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.Frankly ;
    skos:prefLabel "Directional Advice for the (More Than) Human Predicament"@en ;
    skos:definition "Prompted directly by a real, recurring question from viewers — 'I get the big picture, so what do we actually do?' — Nate offers not a checklist but a real, explicit 'compass': 18 directional items organized into 6 broad categories (governance, technology and investing, civic/cultural, ecological, behavioral, and developmental), each framed as a direction to move toward or away from rather than a discrete, achievable solution. Opens with a real, careful distinction between a problem (which has discrete solutions) and a predicament (which does not) — arguing 'the only way out... is through,' requiring hard trade-offs and triage rather than a fix."@en ;
    dct:created "2025-11-18"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2025-11-21"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/frankly-original/114-directional-advice-for-the-more-than-human-predicament> ;
    thinkr:hasReplay <https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=EMKN95xlk7Y> ;
    thinkr:hasTranscript <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/11/Frankly-114-Transcript-Direction-Advice-for-the-More-Than-Human-Predicament.pdf> ;
    thinkr:discusses tgs:Concept.Superorganism, tgs:Concept.IslandsOfCoherence, tgs:Concept.BioticPump ;
    dct:references tgs:Interview.TGS_169_AudreyTang, tgs:Interview.TGS_193_AnastassiaMakarieva ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "Governance: away from single-point fragility (naming nuclear war and uncontrolled AI development directly as the two risks that 'outrank everything else'), toward commons thinking (hard-wiring shared-resource awareness into trade and financial rules), and toward subsidiarity (nested, semi-autonomous local governance that 'bends and doesn't break'). Technology and investing: toward 'Goldilocks tech' (Nate's own real, established checklist — heat pumps over crypto mining, bikes over robotaxis), toward honest pricing (moving taxes off human labor and onto throughput and pollution), and toward wide-boundary capital (a real, proposed simple filter: 'would my grandkids thank me for this investment'). Civic/cultural: away from status built on conspicuous consumption toward status built on stewardship and care, away from polarization toward structured shared-fact deliberation (directly citing Audrey Tang's own real TGS appearance, already built, as a working example), and toward 'islands of coherence' (Nate's own real, recurring concept, already formalized elsewhere in this graph) — including a real, specific proposal to add symbolic local-council seats representing the unborn and keystone species. Ecological: toward active cooling infrastructure, toward including other species as kin and stakeholders rather than background scenery, and toward valuing intact forests and soil directly — citing Anastassia Makarieva's own real TGS appearance (already built) on the biotic pump. Behavioral: away from fear (citing Dune's 'fear is the mind killer' directly) toward what Nate calls 'grounded agency,' toward basic psychological hygiene and 'coalitions of sanity,' and toward individual autonomy from the economic Superorganism — introducing 'rocks in the river' as people who provide stability as surrounding systems destabilize. Developmental: away from self-focus toward a widened sense of identity that includes community and other species, away from consumption-as-identity toward legacy and contribution, and — closing the whole compass — from cleverness toward genuine wisdom, reframing the operative question from 'can we' to 'should we.'"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Real, further citable material found but NOT built in this pass: Nate Soares (real TGS episode #203, directly cited regarding AI extinction risk, now built — 2026-09-02); Daniel Christian Wahl's own real solo TGS episode (#139, directly cited regarding bioregionalism — his Persona already exists in this graph via other appearances, but this specific solo episode is not yet built); Frankly_69 ('Goldilocks Technology — A Preliminary Checklist,' directly cross-referenced as the real origin of this episode's own tech checklist, not built); Frankly_74 ('The Lament of the Bigfoot,' directly cross-referenced regarding 'the agenda of the gene,' not built); Frankly_111 (directly cross-referenced, not built)."@en ;
    .

#############################################
# ADDED 2026-08-31 — Frankly-120 ("The Creature in the Machine"), from
# a complete real transcript + show notes uploaded by MJSullivan.
# Unusually personal — recorded one week after Nate's own real knee
# replacement surgery, during recovery.
#############################################

tgs:Monologue.Frankly_120_TheCreatureInTheMachine a thinkr:Episode, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.Monologue ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.Frankly ;
    skos:prefLabel "The Creature in the Machine"@en ;
    skos:definition "Recorded one week after Nate's own real total knee replacement surgery, this Frankly opens with a real, direct personal observation — 'technically I am now a cyborg' — extending his own established framing of modern humans as already cyborg-like (dependent on external tech for stimuli, memory, and connection) into literal, physical terms. Reflects on real, unscheduled recovery time (his first week without calls in five years) spent reading two books in parallel: Tolkien's The Fellowship of the Ring and Paul Kingsnorth's tgs:Work.AgainstTheMachine, discovering real, direct thematic overlap between them — both, in Nate's own words, concern 'the battle between life and power.'"@en ;
    dct:created "2026-01-21"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2026-01-23"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/frankly-original/120-the-creature-in-the-machine> ;
    thinkr:hasReplay <https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=NEoDH_6BDoU> ;
    thinkr:hasTranscript <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/01/Frankly-120-Transcript-The-Creature-in-the-Machine.pdf> ;
    thinkr:discusses tgs:Concept.Superorganism ;
    dct:references tgs:Work.AgainstTheMachine, tgs:Persona.IainMcGilchrist, tgs:Persona.LewisMumford, tgs:Persona.WendellBerry, tgs:Persona.DanielChristianWahl ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "A real, vivid physical detail opens the reflection: on day three of recovery, a sudden jolt of pain while standing produced what Nate calls a real, direct 'reminder to reside in my body first,' contrasting this against how much of his own work and thinking normally happens in abstraction. Kingsnorth's own real book, per Nate's account, is structured largely around a real Wendell Berry quote (already a built Persona in this graph) on whether humans will choose to remain creatures or become machines — and Nate directly credits the book with revealing how far back this 'machine' critique of civilization actually goes, citing Lewis Mumford (already built in this graph) as an intellectual predecessor he hadn't previously connected to his own Superorganism framework. A real, unscripted political reflection surfaces mid-episode: recorded near a real, current ICE enforcement controversy in Minneapolis, Nate frames the public's sharply divergent interpretations of the same footage not as simple political disagreement but as a real, direct symptom of deeper fractures in how legitimacy, authority, and trust are interpreted — leading him to an open, unresolved question about where governmental power will consolidate as systemic stress increases, and floating bioregionalism (already discussed elsewhere in this graph via Daniel Christian Wahl, already built) as one possible direction. Closes with a real, direct, public personal commitment — never having lifted weights in his life, Nate commits on-camera to three days a week going forward, explicitly noting that stating it publicly to 'tens of thousands' raises the stakes of following through. Closes on a real, paraphrased (not quoted at length, given real copyright limits on Tolkien's text) reflection on Frodo and Gandalf's exchange about not choosing the times one lives through, only what to do with the time given — framed directly as 2026's own likely character, a year when 'decades may happen within weeks.'"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Real, further citable material found but NOT built in this pass: none beyond what's already built or referenced above — this episode's own real citations are unusually concentrated on figures already present in this graph."@en ;
    .

