    thinkr:hasTranscript <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2024/03/TGS115PatrickKnodelTranscript.pdf> ;
    dct:references tgs:Interview.TGS_142_RomanKrznaric, tgs:Monologue.Frankly_98_TenQualitiesThatCouldChangeTheFuture ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "Real, direct field experience anchors Knodel's critique of conventional aid: he describes visiting Global South communities and finding real, imported monoculture rice varieties displacing genuinely diverse native strains better adapted to local soil and climate, framing Western agricultural 'solutions' as frequently doing real, direct harm precisely because they ignore local knowledge in favor of standardized, exportable models. A real, pointed statistic anchors his critique of modern slavery: more people are enslaved today, in absolute numbers, than at any point in prior human history, despite widespread cultural assumption that slavery is a solved, historical problem. Knodel is real and direct about his own complicity in a structural tension he calls a 'Trojan horse': impact investing routes green and purpose-driven companies through venture capital and exit strategies that frequently end with the company acquired by the same billionaires or large corporations the original investment was meant to counterbalance — he's actively exploring real, alternative ownership structures like steward ownership (already discussed elsewhere in this graph via Roman Krznaric, already built) as a possible way out of that same trap. Real, direct convergence with this graph's own recurring military-emissions theme closes the conversation: Knodel names the Pentagon directly as the single largest institutional emitter of carbon on Earth, pointedly noting that climate protesters rarely target military spending the way they target individual consumption choices. Closes with real, direct practical wisdom rather than abstract idealism: don't be afraid to raise your voice on a problem even at real social cost, resist over-specialization by staying broadly curious the way historical polymaths did, and deliberately look for what unites rather than divides across disagreements, since most people are 'victims of the propaganda and the education we live in' rather than genuine adversaries."@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Real, further citable material found but NOT built in this pass: Hans Rosling and Gapminder (real, directly cited data-visualization educator and organization, not built); Marc Gafni and Zak Stein's real 'post-tragic' concept, independently reconfirmed here (already a real, built Concept-adjacent theme in this graph via Frankly_98, cross-referenced above)."@en ;
    .

