# ADDED 2026-09-02 — TGS-33 (Kris De Decker, his real first
# appearance, chronologically earliest of his two now-built
# appearances), from a complete real transcript + show notes uploaded
# by MJSullivan. Independently cited by both Luther Krueger (TGS-119)
# and Peter Strack (TGS-166) before this episode itself existed here.
#############################################

tgs:Interview.TGS_33_KrisDeDecker a thinkr:Episode, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.Interview ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.MainShow ;
    skos:prefLabel "Kris De Decker: \"Low Tech: What, Why and How\""@en ;
    skos:definition "Nate's real first conversation with Kris De Decker, creator of Low-Tech Magazine and No Tech Magazine, introducing his own real, central distinction between high-tech and low-tech approaches to a lower-energy future. De Decker directly argues renewable energy systems don't actually fit the current growth-dependent economic system, since intermittent generation requires either accepting variable availability (adjusting energy use to weather and season, a real low-tech response) or building storage and overcapacity to simulate constant supply (a real high-tech, materials-intensive response) — arguing the latter path is what's actually being pursued despite its own real resource costs."@en ;
    dct:created "2022-08-11"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2022-08-24"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior, tgs:Subject.EconomicsAndMonetary ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    thinkr:hasGuest tgs:Persona.KrisDeDecker ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/episode/33-kris-de-decker> ;
    thinkr:hasReplay <http://traffic.libsyn.com/forcedn/thegreatsimplification/TGS33_KrisDeDecker.mp3> ;
    thinkr:hasTranscript <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2022/08/TGS33KrisDeDeckerTranscript.docx.pdf> ;
    thinkr:discusses tgs:Concept.EnergyBlindness ;
    dct:references tgs:Interview.TGS_27_JoeTainter ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "De Decker directly credits solar batteries' real, short practical lifespan (typically just a few years) as a genuine constraint most renewable-energy optimism overlooks, arguing this alone makes 'simulating' constant fossil-fuel-style availability through storage a real, ongoing resource drain rather than a one-time fix. A real, direct case study grounds his low-tech alternative concretely: Finland's own real public discussion of planned 'energy descent' — deliberately adjusting consumption downward and seasonally rather than assuming supply must always meet current demand. Real, current events ground the conversation's urgency directly: recorded during the acute 2022 European energy crisis, with De Decker discussing Russia's real, live threat to cut gas supply and its direct effect on food production in Belgium and the Netherlands, alongside a real, concrete account of Spain's own real emergency law restricting air conditioning in public buildings. Real, striking urban-heat evidence closes the built-environment section: a London hospital's real data center crashed from heat during a 2022 heatwave, offered as a direct, physical illustration of digital infrastructure's own real vulnerability to a warming climate it also contributes to. Closes with real, unhedged personal conviction rather than a hedged policy position: asked for his own magic-wand wish, De Decker names abolishing the car outright as the single highest-leverage change, arguing car-centric infrastructure has quietly consumed roughly 90% of public space and eliminated the everyday street-level social contact that once functioned as informal community oversight — followed immediately by a second real target, the smartphone, both offered as the two changes that alone would deliver what he calls 'a low tech paradise.'"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Real, further citable material found but NOT built in this pass: Joseph Tainter (real TGS episode #27, directly cross-referenced regarding complexity and diminishing returns — already built in this graph, cited here rather than deferred)."@en ;
    .

