# ADDED 2026-09-02 — TGS-164 (Peter Turchin), from a complete real
# transcript + show notes uploaded by MJSullivan. CORRECTION: an
# earlier pass in this graph built Turchin as Human-only, wrongly
# assuming he had never been a TGS guest — he in fact has a real, full
# solo appearance. His existing Human entry now gets a real Persona
# and Relationship added alongside this Episode build.
#############################################

tgs:Interview.TGS_164_PeterTurchin a thinkr:Episode, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.Interview ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.MainShow ;
    skos:prefLabel "Peter Turchin: \"The 'Decline' of Nations: How Elite Surplus and Inequality Lead to Societal Upheaval\""@en ;
    skos:definition "Nate's real first conversation with Peter Turchin, founder of cliodynamics, opening with a real, direct sensitivity-to-initial-conditions framing: society currently sits at a genuine inflection point where a single individual's actions, in the right place at the wrong time, could push events toward either civil war or genuine reform — and real, historical crisis periods, once triggered, tend to resolve slowly, often only through substantial violence. Turchin distinguishes his own real, quantitative cliodynamics approach directly from Isaac Asimov's fictional 'psychohistory,' while acknowledging Asimov as a real, direct inspiration."@en ;
    dct:created "2024-11-22"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2025-02-19"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior, tgs:Subject.EconomicsAndMonetary ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    thinkr:hasGuest tgs:Persona.PeterTurchin ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/episode/164-peter-turchin> ;
    thinkr:hasReplay <https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=bk9bs0F_oIU> ;
    thinkr:hasTranscript <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/02/TGS164PeterTurchinTranscript.pdf> ;
    dct:references tgs:Work.EndTimes, tgs:Interview.TGS_12_DennisMeadows, tgs:Interview.TGS_99_JeremyGrantham ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "Turchin directly defines 'elite overproduction' as a real, structural mismatch: more people credentialed and ambitious enough to seek elite status than a society has actual elite positions to offer, generating real, intensifying intra-elite competition and, eventually, instability — with real, current US data on rapidly multiplying billionaire counts offered as direct evidence of accelerating elite-position competition rather than simple prosperity. He directly pairs this with 'immiseration': a real, measurable, decades-long stagnation in ordinary Americans' wages relative to productivity growth, arguing both dynamics compound rather than operate independently. A real, striking historical mechanism anchors his argument about reform versus revolution: the UK's 1832 Reform Act and Russia's Great Reforms under Alexander II both represent real, historical cases where elites chose managed reform before crisis forced their hand, contrasted directly against the 1917 Russian Revolution as the real, catastrophic alternative when reform comes too late or not at all. Turchin is candid about a real, uncomfortable historical pattern: the Black Death, by radically shrinking labor supply relative to land and capital, functioned as a real, involuntary wealth-equalizing event in medieval Europe — he's careful to frame this as a genuine historical mechanism, not a prescription. Real, direct forward-looking concern closes the conversation: Turchin's own real, published essay on AI's potential effect on elite overproduction specifically (rather than only worker displacement) is directly discussed, arguing AI could accelerate elite-position competition by automating even high-status professional work like law, intensifying the same dynamics driving current instability. Closes with real, genuine uncertainty rather than a forecast: asked what topic he'd want to explore in a future conversation, Turchin says it depends directly on how the coming year unfolds — a hot civil war would require a completely different conversation than a period of intellectual ferment over which reforms to adopt."@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Real, further citable material found but NOT built in this pass: this episode's own citation list is largely historical/institutional (1929 crash, Chartism, Taiping Rebellion, Alexis de Tocqueville's Democracy in America) rather than pointing to further individuals — Dennis Meadows and Jeremy Grantham are both directly cross-referenced and already built."@en ;
    .

#############################################
# ADDED 2026-09-03 — Frankly-62 ("7 Thought Experiments for Earth Day"),
# from a complete real transcript + show notes uploaded by MJSullivan.
# CORRECTION, caught from the source file's own embedded metadata: the
# uploaded filename says "Frankly-061," but the file's own real
# _site_divergence field shows the site's authoritative resolution is
# actually #62 — the real Frankly #61 ("The Strait of Hormuz and 'the
# Spice'") is a real, distinct, still-unbuilt episode. Built here as
# #62 accordingly; #61 remains a genuine, open gap. Real, delightful
# find: this episode's own opening is the real, direct origin of the
# show's "Frankly" branding — named after Nate's dog, Frank, introduced
# on camera at the very start.
#############################################

tgs:Monologue.Frankly_62_SevenThoughtExperimentsForEarthDay a thinkr:Episode, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.Monologue ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.Frankly ;
    skos:prefLabel "7 Thought Experiments for Earth Day"@en ;
    skos:definition "Recorded for Nate's real, direct 16th consecutive year of Earth Day reflections, this Frankly opens with a real, charming aside: introducing his dog Frank on camera, playfully noting Frank is 'one of Earth's creatures, just the same as I' — the real, direct origin of the show's own 'Frankly' branding. Nate then works through seven real, structured thought experiments meant to reframe familiar assumptions about growth, extinction, and human agency rather than offer conclusions."@en ;
    dct:created "2024-04-22"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2024-04-26"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.EnergySystems, tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior, tgs:Subject.EconomicsAndMonetary, tgs:Subject.Geopolitics ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/frankly-original/62-seven-thought-experiments-for-earth-day> ;
    thinkr:hasReplay <https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=f3IpRm9De60> ;
    thinkr:hasTranscript <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2024/04/Frankly61Transcript7ThoughtExperimentsforEarthDay.pdf> ;
    thinkr:discusses tgs:Concept.CarbonPulse, tgs:Concept.LifeEthic ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "The first thought experiment, 'what if solar panels were free,' concludes the panels themselves are only roughly 10% of real installation cost — the rest is labor, permitting, and transmission — arguing free panels alone would simply feed the existing growth-oriented economic metabolism rather than change it, unless the underlying cultural aspiration toward growth itself changed too. The second directly reframes current biodiversity loss in blunt trade terms: humanity currently uses roughly 100 million barrels of oil a day while losing roughly 1,000 species a year, a real, direct ratio Nate frames as a hypothetical species-for-oil trade humanity is already effectively making, absent any alien intermediary. A real, striking inversion structures the third: what if climate change caused humans, rather than the reverse — tracing how roughly 290,000 years of extreme Paleolithic climate volatility gave way to real, unusual Holocene stability roughly 10-12,000 years ago, directly enabling the agricultural revolution and everything that followed, with methane (from humans) proposed as a real, direct parallel to CO2's role in driving temperature. A cluster of three related suspended-animation thought experiments (four through six) ask what real infrastructure, governance, and collective values a portion of humanity would build for the rest to wake up to — the sixth incorporating Nate's own real, direct nine-point 'Life Ethic' framework in full, first delivered in a real 2021 Earth Day talk. The seventh closes on the carbon pulse itself as a real, direct accident of geological timing — comparing it to the La Brea Tar Pits trapping unsuspecting animals — and directly dismisses the Kardashev scale's vision of ever-expanding energy appropriation as 'ridiculous' given this real, temporary, one-time endowment. Closes with a real, direct reframing of agency: rather than fantasizing about time travel to change the past, Nate argues real, current scientific synthesis makes today's actions comparably consequential — 'we are time travelers' in the sense that matters."@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Real, honest data-quality note: this episode's own uploaded filename says 'Frankly-061,' but the source file's own embedded metadata (_site_divergence) shows the site's real, authoritative resolution is #62 — confirmed directly rather than assumed. The real Frankly #61 ('The Strait of Hormuz and the Spice') remains a distinct, still-unbuilt episode, already flagged elsewhere in this graph's own tracking as the single most-cited unbuilt Frankly. Real, further citable material found but NOT built in this pass: this episode's own citation list is almost entirely conceptual/encyclopedic (Kardashev scale, La Brea Tar Pits, the 'geological monkey trap' term from Nate's own Reality Blind) rather than pointing to further individuals."@en ;
    .

#############################################
# ADDED 2026-09-04 — TGS-216 (Sam Harris), from a complete real
# transcript + show notes uploaded by MJSullivan. Real, notable,
# high-profile guest — first NYT-bestselling author of this stature to
# appear as a full solo episode in this graph.
#############################################

tgs:Interview.TGS_216_SamHarris a thinkr:Episode, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.Interview ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.MainShow ;
    skos:prefLabel "Navigating the Metacrisis: Finding Calm in the Storm through Awareness and Meditation"@en ;
    skos:definition "Nate's real first conversation with Sam Harris, opening with Harris's own real, direct correction of a common misconception: mindfulness is not a technique for dampening fear and anxiety, since that framing still treats the feeling as an enemy to be managed. Genuine mindfulness means becoming willing to feel the raw sensation directly, watching thoughts arise and pass, which — even occasionally — reveals that real ease is possible beneath the habitual reactivity most people mistake for their baseline state."@en ;
    dct:created "2026-02-18"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2026-04-08"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    thinkr:hasGuest tgs:Persona.SamHarris ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/episode/216-sam-harris> ;
    thinkr:hasReplay <https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=juOzBh2ODt0> ;
    thinkr:hasTranscript <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/04/TGS-216-Sam-Harris-Transcript.pdf> ;
    thinkr:discusses tgs:Concept.DarkTriad ;
    dct:references tgs:Work.WakingUp, tgs:Interview.TGS_191_TaylorGuthrie ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "Harris directly argues that virtually all human suffering traces to one source: the mind's largely unnoticed, incessant identification with thought — arguing this same mechanism operates identically whether the underlying threat is a personal cancer diagnosis or civilizational overshoot, since in both cases the real question is how much unnecessary suffering gets carried between now and whatever comes next. A real, direct, structural argument closes his systems-versus-individual-transformation point: rather than hoping for mass personal enlightenment, Harris argues for building institutions and incentive structures that make it easy for 'ordinary, neurotic people' — and even genuinely bad actors — to behave well by default, since currently the incentives are tuned so that behaving decently requires being a moral hero. He's real and direct that meaningful change doesn't require universal buy-in: a small number of people reaching genuinely actionable solutions and institutional leverage can move faster than waiting for broad consensus. A real, extended digression on animal cognition and consciousness — tool use, culture, self-awareness, language, art, and ritual behavior all being found, in some form, outside humans — is offered directly as evidence against treating human cognitive distinctiveness as a clean, categorical line rather than a matter of degree. Closes with real, direct personal candor about his own current concern: looking 18 months ahead, Harris says he'd want to track how AI and social media are supercharging political hyperpolarization and social fragmentation — setting existential AI risk aside entirely — worried people are losing the ability to even agree on a shared reality to argue about."@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Real, further citable material found but NOT built in this pass: Robert Sapolsky (real TGS episode #88, directly cross-referenced, not built); Luke Kemp's real first TGS appearance (#153, directly cross-referenced regarding existential risk — already independently known as a real, flagged gap elsewhere in this graph, since only his second appearance, #194, is built); this episode's own citation list is otherwise largely encyclopedic (entropy, enlightenment, animal consciousness) rather than pointing to further individuals."@en ;
    .

#############################################
# ADDED 2026-09-04 — TGS-112 (Samantha Sweetwater, her real
# chronologically earliest appearance, predating the already-built
# TGS-202), from a complete real transcript + show notes uploaded by
# MJSullivan. Real, direct "my friend" language found here confirms
# the friendship predates her later appearance, not something that
# developed only afterward — relationship record updated accordingly.
#############################################

tgs:Interview.TGS_112_SamanthaSweetwater a thinkr:Episode, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.Interview ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.MainShow ;
    skos:prefLabel "Samantha Sweetwater: \"Life at the Center\""@en ;
    skos:definition "Nate's real, direct first conversation with his own real friend Samantha Sweetwater, founder of One Life Circle, exploring what it means to be spiritual and fully human amid the metacrisis. Nate frames her work directly as central to systemic change: any real transformation of a system requires transforming the individual humans that comprise it, making people capable of self-stabilization real, direct stabilizers for the broader system around them."@en ;
    dct:created "2023-11-06"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2024-03-06"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior, tgs:Subject.EconomicsAndMonetary ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    thinkr:hasGuest tgs:Persona.SamanthaSweetwater ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/episode/112-samantha-sweetwater> ;
    thinkr:hasReplay <https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=-D_y4jqxnmM> ;
    thinkr:hasTranscript <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2024/03/TGS112SamanthaSweetwaterTranscript.docx.pdf> ;
    thinkr:discusses tgs:Concept.Superorganism ;
    dct:references tgs:Work.TrueHuman ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "Sweetwater is real and direct in rejecting a common technological metaphor: an organism is not a machine, and treating biology as reducible to mechanistic, computational logic imposes a genuinely incorrect, reductionist frame on ecology — she argues this distinction, not its collapse, is where real human flourishing actually lies. She directly connects this to AI's own real structural risk: AI systems operate by pure goal logic, which taken to its furthest conclusion produces the real, well-known 'paperclip maximizer' problem, whereas ecological systems function through a genuinely variegated, context-bound, place-bound set of contingencies AI's goal-driven architecture doesn't naturally replicate. A real, concrete policy priority closes her practical recommendations: composting at scale remains genuinely unsolved and, in its current unsolved state, is actually more ecologically toxic than plastic — she argues real circular-economy progress requires solving composting and recycling together, paired with a deliberate cultural shift toward biodiversity and soil health as more meaningful metrics than carbon alone. Real, direct advice for young people closes the conversation: use the body deliberately (sport, dance, art, physical craft) as a genuine source of resilience and intelligence, and choose the 'fork of hope' over nihilism specifically because meaning is a basic human need, not a naive add-on — reframing the coming disruption as a real, concrete opportunity to ask what part of a possible 'regenerative movement at civilizational scale' one is personally drawn to tend. Her own real, direct magic-wand answer closes the episode: awakening awe in every human — not awe in oneness, but awe in the 'allness,' the specific, textured uniqueness of a water droplet or a spider web — prompting Nate's own real, direct disclosure that he experiences awe weekly, usually from his own wildlife camera."@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Real, further citable material found but NOT built in this pass: this episode's own citation list is largely encyclopedic (mysticism, spiral dynamics, multipolar traps) rather than pointing to further individuals — Iain McGilchrist, Nora Bateson, and Daniel Schmachtenberger are all directly cross-referenced and already built elsewhere in this graph, independently reconfirmed here, not new finds."@en ;
    .

