# ADDED 2026-09-04 — TGS-219 (Tad Patzek, his real chronologically
# earliest solo appearance, predating the already-built TGS-225), from
# a complete real transcript + show notes uploaded by MJSullivan.
# Directly on-topic for the real WWNS Topic 2 (Overshoot/biophysical
# limits) coverage gap.
#############################################

tgs:Interview.TGS_219_TadPatzek a thinkr:Episode, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.Interview ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.MainShow ;
    skos:prefLabel "Why Each American Lives Like a 40-Ton Whale: Power, Overshoot, and Climate"@en ;
    skos:definition "Nate's real, direct first conversation with his own real, ~20-year friend Tad Patzek, thermodynamicist and earth scientist, opening with Patzek's own real, central argument: public discourse about energy operates almost entirely at the level of policy, politics, and moral aspiration, while remaining dangerously detached from the underlying physical infrastructure actually moving power and materials — and by the time the resulting biosphere damage becomes visible, it's already structurally locked in. Patzek's own real, vivid central image: an average American continuously consumes roughly 10,000 watts of power, the real metabolic equivalent of a 40-ton sperm whale, against a human body's own real baseline output of about 100 watts."@en ;
    dct:created "2026-03-11"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2026-05-06"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.EcologyAndEnvironment ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    thinkr:hasGuest tgs:Persona.TadPatzek ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/episode/219-tad-patzek> ;
    thinkr:hasReplay <https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=BXR94APZ31Q> ;
    thinkr:hasTranscript <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/05/TGS-219-Tad-Patzek-Transcript-1.pdf> ;
    thinkr:discusses tgs:Concept.CarbonPulse ;
    dct:references tgs:Work.ThermalPowerAndClimateChange, tgs:Work.DrillingDown, tgs:Interview.TGS_27_JoeTainter ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "Patzek directly frames climate change and overpopulation as downstream consequences of one real, underlying variable: the sheer surplus energy available to humanity, not as separate crises with separate causes. A real, historical throughline anchors his 'energy transitions are additive, not subtractive' argument: England's real, near-total dependence on coal by the 1800s directly parallels the US's real dependence on fossil fuels by 2010 — in his own real, direct account, every subsequent energy source in history has been layered on top of prior ones, increasing total power flowing through the system, rather than genuinely replacing what came before, meaning current renewable buildout should be expected to follow the same real, additive pattern rather than a clean substitution. He directly credits his own real, co-authored book with Joseph Tainter (already built), examining the Deepwater Horizon disaster, as the direct intellectual bridge between his own thermodynamic focus and Tainter's own real theory of societal collapse via diminishing returns on complexity. A real, extended, direct treatment of AI closes the conversation's contemporary-relevance section: Patzek discusses AI's real, substantial energy demand (individual data centers drawing 100-300 megawatts each), its real embodied-materials footprint, and a real, direct concern about AI being used by elites to further concentrate political power and centralize decision-making — treating AI's environmental and political risks as structurally linked, not separate concerns. Real, striking historical scale comparisons close the geopolitical section: the Roman Empire's own real historical geographic extent, and just-in-time global supply chains' real vulnerability as a single point of failure, both offered as concrete precedent for how complex systems built on surplus energy eventually confront real, hard limits. Closes with real, direct practical advice rather than abstract theory: learn real science through free resources like Khan Academy, deliberately practice manual skills and making things, and — Patzek's own real, direct parting request — download his own free, hyperlinked PDF book and follow its links at one's own pace rather than digest it linearly."@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Real, further citable material found but NOT built in this pass: this episode's own citation list is otherwise almost entirely encyclopedic/institutional (thermodynamics, exponential growth, degrowth) rather than pointing to further individuals — Joseph Tainter is directly cross-referenced and already built, not a new find."@en ;
    .

#############################################
# ADDED 2026-09-04 — TGS-18 (Thomas Murphy), from a complete real
# transcript + show notes uploaded by MJSullivan. Directly on-topic
# for the real WWNS Topic 2 (Overshoot/biophysical limits) coverage
# gap — one of the most direct, explicit friendship confirmations
# found this session.
#############################################

tgs:Interview.TGS_18_ThomasMurphy a thinkr:Episode, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.Interview ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.MainShow ;
    skos:prefLabel "Thomas Murphy: \"Physics and Planetary Ambitions\""@en ;
    skos:definition "Nate's real conversation with his own real friend Thomas Murphy, physics professor at UC San Diego and author of the real, long-running 'Do the Math' blog, working through why continued energy growth is a physical, not merely political or economic, impossibility. Murphy's own real, striking calculation: at current growth rates, humanity's total energy consumption would exceed the entire galaxy's energy output within just a few thousand years — a real, direct demonstration that exponential growth in a finite physical system cannot continue indefinitely, regardless of the energy source."@en ;
    dct:created "2022-03-15"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2022-05-12"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior, tgs:Subject.EconomicsAndMonetary ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    thinkr:hasGuest tgs:Persona.ThomasMurphy ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/episode/18-tom-murphy> ;
    thinkr:hasReplay <https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=KXertTq0knU> ;
    thinkr:hasTranscript <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2022/05/TGS18ThomasMurphyTranscript.docx.pdf> ;
    thinkr:discusses tgs:Concept.CarbonPulse ;
    dct:references tgs:Work.EnergyAndHumanAmbitions, tgs:Interview.TGS_6_HermanDaly ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "Murphy directly grounds his argument in basic thermodynamics: physical growth of any kind, including energy use, is fundamentally bound by the laws governing energy and matter, meaning economic 'decoupling' from physical throughput is a real, direct violation of physics if pursued indefinitely rather than a genuine escape route. A real, striking waste-heat calculation closes his energy-limits argument: continued 2-3% annual energy growth would, within a surprisingly short historical timeframe, generate enough waste heat alone to boil the oceans, independent of any greenhouse gas effect whatsoever — offered as a real, direct proof that energy growth itself, not just its byproducts, has hard physical limits. He's real and direct about a psychological mechanism underlying collective inaction: humans are, in his own real phrase, 'programmed to ignore' slow-moving, abstract threats, a real cognitive tendency he connects directly to the discount rate humans intuitively apply to distant future harm. Murphy is candid and unusually direct about his own emotional relationship to collapse risk: having already moved through real stages of grief regarding civilization's trajectory, he states plainly that earlier collapse would leave more real resources available for recovery than continued 'successful' delay — arguing continued short-term success at avoiding collapse is actually the most dangerous possible outcome, since it only compounds the eventual overshoot. Closes on real, considered hope distinct from naive optimism: while worried younger generations understand growth's limits abstractly but still direct their concern outward ('the billionaire's problem, not their own habits'), Murphy expresses real, genuine longer-term hope in human adaptability — arguing someone born into a post-collapse world would simply experience it as normal, and could, in a real sense, do just fine."@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Real, further citable material found but NOT built in this pass: this episode's own citation list is largely encyclopedic/institutional (thermodynamics, exponential growth, Myers-Briggs) rather than pointing to further individuals — Herman Daly is directly cross-referenced and already built, not a new find; Thomas Malthus (real, directly cited, historical figure, not built)."@en ;
    .

