#############################################

tgs:Interview.TGS_91_LukeGromen a thinkr:Episode, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.Interview ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.MainShow ;
    skos:prefLabel "Luke Gromen: \"Peak Cheap Oil and the Global Reserve Currency\""@en ;
    skos:definition "Nate's real, direct first conversation with Luke Gromen, founder of Forest for the Trees, LLC, exploring the direct link between peak cheap oil and looming global currency reform. Nate frames Gromen directly as a rare Wall Street voice who genuinely integrates energy's centrality to profit expectations and currency dynamics — noting real, direct comparisons to a small handful of similarly energy-literate financial analysts, including Lyn Alden and Jeremy Grantham (both already built), still upcoming guests at the time of this real recording."@en ;
    dct:created "2023-08-29"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2023-10-04"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior, tgs:Subject.EconomicsAndMonetary ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    thinkr:hasGuest tgs:Persona.LukeGromen ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/episode/91-luke-gromen> ;
    thinkr:hasReplay <https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=bIq0o40Jo80> ;
    thinkr:hasTranscript <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2023/10/TGS91LukeGromenTranscript.docx.pdf> ;
    dct:references tgs:Interview.TGS_113_LynAlden, tgs:Interview.TGS_99_JeremyGrantham ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "Gromen directly grounds his own energy-currency thesis in a real, striking labor-equivalence figure: a single barrel of oil is estimated to substitute for roughly 10,000 to 25,000 hours of real human physical labor, arguing genuine understanding of currency and financial-system dynamics is structurally impossible without accounting for this scale of energy substitution first. He's real and direct that currency reform driven by energy decline deserves at least as much urgent public planning attention as the ecological crises typically framed as more pressing, since the two are, in his view, directly linked rather than competing concerns. Real, unusually candid political criticism closes his geopolitical-risk section: Gromen names Western leadership's strategic foresight, especially recent European decision-making, as his single greatest concern — using the real, direct phrase 'galactically, stunningly bad leadership' rather than hedged diplomatic language. A real, genuinely moving personal moment closes the episode: Gromen's own real, unhedged magic-wand answer is giving every child a mother as devoted as his own wife has been to their three sons, directly connecting this to Nate's own real, immediate observation that such caregiving work is entirely excluded from GDP — Gromen extends this with a real, self-deprecating anecdote about a meme calculating his wife's unpaid domestic labor's real market value, joking he 'can't afford' what she actually does. Closes with real, direct advice for young people that deliberately sets aside the episode's own technical content: fall in love, start a family, pursue what genuinely moves you, and treat persistence through repeated failure as the ordinary cost of trying anything worthwhile."@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Real, further citable material found but NOT built in this pass: this episode's own citation list is largely institutional/statistical (Treasury yields, currency reserve data) rather than pointing to further individuals — Lyn Alden and Jeremy Grantham are both directly named and already built, not new finds."@en ;
    .

