# ADDED 2026-09-04 — TGS-17 (Chuck Watson), his real second solo TGS
# appearance (chronologically his third real conversation with Nate,
# following TGS-4 and an unbuilt Frankly), from a complete real
# transcript + show notes uploaded by MJSullivan. Prompted directly
# by real listener feedback requesting a deeper dive on nuclear risk.
#############################################

tgs:Interview.TGS_17_ChuckWatson a thinkr:Episode, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.Interview ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.MainShow ;
    skos:prefLabel "Chuck Watson: \"Nuclear War – All the Questions You Were Afraid to Ask\""@en ;
    skos:definition "Nate's real, direct third conversation with Chuck Watson, prompted specifically by real listener requests for a deeper technical dive on nuclear risk. Nate directly introduces Watson as someone who 'never calls attention to himself,' and discloses learning real, new things about him even after 15 years of friendship — framing Watson's willingness to speak publicly on this topic as a deliberate, considered choice given his own real, natural reticence."@en ;
    dct:created "2022-04-28"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2022-05-04"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior, tgs:Subject.EconomicsAndMonetary ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    thinkr:hasGuest tgs:Persona.ChuckWatson ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/episode/17-chuck-watson-nuclear-war> ;
    thinkr:hasReplay <https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=hvXO_rHbS9c> ;
    thinkr:hasTranscript <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2022/05/TGS17ChuckWatsonTranscript.docx.pdf> ;
    thinkr:discusses tgs:Concept.SupernormalStimuli, tgs:Concept.Superorganism ;
    dct:references tgs:Interview.TGS_4_ChuckWatson, tgs:Interview.TGS_214_TristanHarris ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "Watson directly walks through the real technical distinction between fission and fusion weapons, and real, specific concepts like 'dial-a-yield' (modern weapons whose explosive yield can be adjusted before deployment) — arguing this real, increased flexibility paradoxically makes nuclear weapons feel more usable, not less dangerous. A real, direct extension of Nate's own established 'supernormal stimuli' teaching concept (now formalized as a Concept in this graph) emerges organically mid-conversation: Nate proposes nuclear weapons themselves, not just money, as the largest real supernormal stimulus humans have created, given their real, many-orders-of-magnitude destructive scale relative to any ancestral weapon. A real, genuinely vulnerable personal disclosure closes the conversation: Watson admits that professionals in his own field must partially 'shut off' their humanity to think clearly about targeting and blast calculations, describing this as real, personally frightening — crediting a real, direct visit to Beirut and witnessing the aftermath of a comparatively 'trivial' conventional conflict with helping restore his own perspective on nuclear war's real, vastly greater stakes. Watson's own real, direct definition of patriotism closes the episode, quoting 19th-century senator Carl Schurz directly: genuine patriotism means ensuring one's own country does what's right, not reflexive loyalty regardless of conduct."@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Real, further citable material found but NOT built in this pass: Tristan Harris's real first TGS appearance (already directly cross-referenced in this episode's own show notes) remains unbuilt — only his second appearance, TGS_214, exists in this graph; this episode's own citation list is otherwise largely encyclopedic/institutional (MIRV, Proud Prophet, Intermediate Nuclear Forces Treaty) rather than pointing to further individuals."@en ;
    .

