    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "Millison directly grounds permaculture in real, physical geomorphology — reading land contours and water flow patterns as the real, foundational starting point for any design, rather than imposing a generic template regardless of terrain. He's real and direct about his own extensive documented fieldwork, drawing on real, direct project experience across India, Egypt, Mexico, Cuba, and the US, offered as concrete, geographically diverse proof that permaculture principles genuinely transfer across radically different climates and cultures rather than working only in ideal conditions. A real, direct preview closes the conversation: Millison discusses his own real, then-forthcoming documentary series on India's 'water revolution,' having just returned from filming there — later expanded into the real, extensive body of work directly cited in his own later TGS appearance (already built, TGS-178)."@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Real, further citable material found but NOT built in this pass: this episode's own citation list is otherwise almost entirely encyclopedic/practical (permaculture design principles, water harvesting techniques) rather than pointing to further individuals."@en ;
