    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    thinkr:hasGuest tgs:Persona.VandanaShiva ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/episode/46-vandana-shiva> ;
    dct:references tgs:PanelDiscussion.RR_6_FossilFreeFoodSystems ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "Shiva directly argues industrial agriculture's real productivity claims are structurally misleading, since they typically measure yield per single crop rather than genuine total nutritional output per acre of a real, diverse polyculture — a real, foundational critique underlying her broader agroecology advocacy. She's real and direct about her own real, four-decade institutional commitment through the Research Foundation for Science, Technology and Ecology, framing her work as directly opposing what she considers a real, deliberate narrowing of both biological and cognitive diversity by industrial and corporate agricultural interests. Closes on a real, warm, human note distinct from the conversation's substantive weight: Shiva directly invites Nate to visit her in India, and the two close by discussing the real, specific meal she's about to eat — capsicum, potato, and chapati — offered as a small, genuine moment of human connection after a wide-ranging discussion."@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Real, further citable material found but NOT built in this pass: this episode's own citation list is otherwise almost entirely encyclopedic (agroecology, biodiversity, seed sovereignty) rather than pointing to further individuals."@en ;
    .

