# ADDED 2026-09-04 — TGS-76 (Daniel Zetah), his real solo TGS
# appearance, from a complete real transcript + show notes uploaded
# by MJSullivan. Directly on-topic for the real WWNS Topic 9
# (Community/localization) gap.
#############################################

tgs:Interview.TGS_76_DanielZetah a thinkr:Episode, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.Interview ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.MainShow ;
    skos:prefLabel "Daniel Zetah: \"Regenerative Agriculture and Personal Sovereignty\""@en ;
    skos:definition "Nate's real, direct first conversation with his own real, geographically nearby friend Daniel Zetah — living just west of Minneapolis, close to Nate himself — who farms with his wife Stephanie on the family farm where they grow roughly 80% of their own calories. Nate directly frames this as 'a new direction' for the show, deliberately shifting toward practical response rather than only diagnosis, introducing Zetah's own real, coined concept of 'personal sovereignty' as a genuine, livable alternative to conventional systemic dependence."@en ;
    dct:created "2023-05-17"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2023-06-21"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior, tgs:Subject.EconomicsAndMonetary ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    thinkr:hasGuest tgs:Persona.DanielZetah ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/episode/76-daniel-zetah> ;
    thinkr:hasReplay <https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=PtW_Fh9fViw> ;
    thinkr:hasTranscript <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2023/06/TGS76DanielZetahTranscript.docx-1.pdf> ;
    dct:references tgs:PanelDiscussion.RR_6_FossilFreeFoodSystems, tgs:Work.Ishmael ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "Zetah directly grounds his own real critique of industrial agriculture in stark soil-carbon numbers: pre-settlement Midwest soils held roughly 12-15% organic carbon, now down to under 2%, with regenerative practices capable of rebuilding roughly 0.75-1% carbon content per year — a real, concrete, measurable path back, not merely a qualitative aspiration. He's real and direct that his own real, unconventional life path — 15 years living abroad, including stints as a snake relocator and chemical-spraying auditor in Tasmania, before real, direct time in an off-grid Amish-adjacent community — was itself the necessary preparation for genuinely understanding what 'personal sovereignty' requires in practice, not something he arrived at through theory alone. A real, direct rebuttal to the common ecological-guilt narrative around cattle closes the animal-agriculture section: Zetah argues regeneratively-raised cows produce measurably less methane than CAFO-raised cattle and can function as real, direct carbon-sequestration tools when managed in a genuinely regenerative rotational system, rather than being an inherent environmental liability. Closes with real, direct, unfiltered emotional urgency rather than hedged messaging: Zetah states plainly that 'the time for playing around is over,' directly naming widespread unhappiness within the very system most people continue participating in, before his own real, unguarded answer on what he cares about most — 'life in general' — and his own real, direct fear: not extinction itself, but humanity finding a way to technologically persist inside a genuinely diminished, biodiversity-stripped world."@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Real, further citable material found but NOT built in this pass: this episode's own citation list is otherwise largely encyclopedic/institutional (soil carbon studies, CAFO reporting, indigenous food systems) rather than pointing to further individuals — Robert Lustig and Joslin Faith Kehdy are both directly cross-referenced and already built elsewhere in this graph, not new finds."@en ;
    .

