# ADDED 2026-09-04 — TGS-104 (Jane Muncke), her real chronologically
# earliest appearance, predating her already-built TGS-154, from a
# complete real transcript + show notes uploaded by MJSullivan.
# Directly on-topic for the real WWNS Topic 6 (Ecology) coverage.
#############################################

tgs:Interview.TGS_104_JaneMuncke a thinkr:Episode, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.Interview ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.MainShow ;
    skos:prefLabel "Jane Muncke: \"Perils of Plastic Packaging\""@en ;
    skos:definition "Nate's real, direct first conversation with his own real friend Jane Muncke, Chief Scientific Officer of the Food Packaging Forum, opening directly with her own real, pointed regulatory critique: Europe's food-contact-material rules are comparatively progressive on paper but genuinely under-enforced, meaning European citizens are not, in practice, meaningfully better protected from packaging chemicals than people elsewhere. The conversation is framed as a real, direct follow-up to Jeremy Grantham's own already-built TGS appearance, in which Grantham argued endocrine-disrupting chemicals may pose a genuinely larger threat to humanity than climate change itself."@en ;
    dct:created "2023-11-01"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2024-01-10"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior, tgs:Subject.EconomicsAndMonetary ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    thinkr:hasGuest tgs:Persona.JaneMuncke ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/episode/104-jane-muncke> ;
    thinkr:hasReplay <https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=I2-roqSWjFo> ;
    thinkr:hasTranscript <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2024/01/TGS104JaneMunckeTranscript.pdf> ;
    dct:references tgs:Interview.TGS_99_JeremyGrantham ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "Muncke directly explains real 'chemical migration' — the transfer of packaging chemicals directly into food, accelerated by heat, fat content, and storage duration — as a genuinely under-recognized exposure pathway distinct from more publicized concerns like visible plastic waste. She's real and direct about a genuine structural lock-in: food-packaging machinery is expensive, highly product-specific, and slow to earn back its investment, creating real, direct economic disincentive against switching materials even once real health risks become known. A real, pointed critique of recycling-as-solution anchors her own systemic argument: glass demonstrates genuine circularity, while plastic degrades in quality with each real recycling cycle, meaning 'recyclable' labeling often functions more as a real, direct consumer-reassurance mechanism than an accurate description of actual material fate. Muncke directly connects packaging chemical exposure to broader chronic-disease trends, framing food-contact materials as a real, underappreciated contributor within the larger real, documented rise in global chronic illness. Closes with a real, direct combination of professional and playful answers: her own real, unhedged magic-wand response is fully safe food-contact materials containing no known-hazardous or untested chemicals — followed immediately by a real, more personal alternative: requiring everyone to spend at least one or two days a year actually growing their own food, to restore genuine appreciation for both its difficulty and its beauty."@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Real, further citable material found but NOT built in this pass: Frederick vom Saal (real, directly cited BPA researcher, not built); this episode's own citation list is otherwise largely encyclopedic/institutional (endocrine disruption, ultra-processed food, chemical migration studies) rather than pointing to further individuals."@en ;
    .

